var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Analysis-API","page":"Analysis","text":"The analysis layer provides inference tools that treat experiments as black boxes. It is composed of three source files:\n\nanalysis_tools.jl — core types, parameter utilities, likelihood generation, scanning, profiling, and data generation. All symbols are part of the Newtrinos module.\nmolewhacker.jl — adaptive importance sampling via MGVI Gaussian mixture refinement. All symbols are part of the Newtrinos module.\ncli_common.jl — helper functions for CLI scripts. These are standalone utilities loaded via include and are not exported from the Newtrinos module.\n\n","title":"Analysis API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Parameter-Utilities","page":"Analysis","text":"These functions collect and merge parameters and priors across physics and experiment modules. condition and correlated_priors_vars allow fixing or correlating individual parameters before constructing a posterior.\n\n","title":"Parameter Utilities"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Data-Generation","page":"Analysis","text":"","title":"Data Generation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Adaptive-Importance-Sampling","page":"Analysis","text":"The molewhacker tools implement an adaptive importance sampling strategy. Starting from an initial Gaussian mixture proposal, each iteration fits a new local MGVI Gaussian component at the highest-weight sample point and adds it to the mixture.  This \"whack-a-mole\" strategy adaptively targets undersampled regions of the posterior. whack_many_moles is the recommended entry point for production runs. \n\n","title":"Adaptive Importance Sampling"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Likelihood-Construction","page":"Analysis","text":"","title":"Likelihood Construction"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Scanning-and-Profiling","page":"Analysis","text":"scan evaluates the joint likelihood on a fixed grid without optimization. profile minimizes over nuisance parameters at each grid point. find_mle / find_mle_cached locate the global maximum-likelihood estimate.\n\n","title":"Scanning and Profiling"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Utilities","page":"Analysis","text":"","title":"Utilities"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Internal-API","page":"Analysis","text":"Low-level helpers used internally by the scanning and profiling pipeline.\n\n","title":"Internal API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#CLI-Usage","page":"Analysis","text":"","title":"CLI Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#analysis.jl","page":"Analysis","text":"src/analysis/analysis.jl is the main command-line entry point for running analyses. It is invoked with julia --project:\n\njulia --project src/analysis/analysis.jl \\\n  --experiments deepcore dayabay \\\n  --name myrun \\\n  --task scan","title":"analysis.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Command-line-Arguments","page":"Analysis","text":"Argument Type Required Description\n--experiments String... Yes Space-separated experiment names (e.g. deepcore dayabay)\n--name String Yes Base name for output files\n--task String Yes One of Scan, Profile, ImportanceSampling, NestedSampling\n--plot flag No If set, render and save a PNG plot of the result\n--workers Int No Number of distributed workers (default: 1)\n--threads Int No Threads per worker when --workers > 1 (default: 1)","title":"Command-line Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Task-Modes","page":"Analysis","text":"scan — evaluates the joint likelihood on a fixed parameter grid defined in the script body. No nuisance-parameter optimization. Fast.\nprofile — minimizes over nuisance parameters at each grid point via L-BFGS. Supports distributed execution via --workers. Results are checkpointed per row.\nimportancesampling — runs whack_many_moles to produce importance-weighted posterior samples from a Gaussian mixture approximation.\nnestedsampling — runs ReactiveNestedSampling from UltraNest.","title":"Task Modes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Output-Files","page":"Analysis","text":"File Contents\n<name>.jld2 NewtrinosResult (scan/profile) or sample dict (sampling)\n<name>.png Plot of the result (only with --plot)\n<name>/ Per-iteration checkpoint directory (profile and importance sampling)","title":"Output Files"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Physics-Configuration","page":"Analysis","text":"To use non-default oscillation models (inverted ordering, sterile neutrinos, custom flux, etc.), uncomment and edit the override block near the top of the script.","title":"Physics Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Helper-Functions-(cli_common.jl)","page":"Analysis","text":"The following functions are defined in src/analysis/cli_common.jl and loaded by CLI scripts via include. They are not exported from the Newtrinos module.\n\n\n\nconfigure_experiments(experiment_list)\n\nConfigure a list of experiments using each experiment's built-in defaults. Looks up each name in the Newtrinos module (case-insensitive), calls its configure() method, and returns a NamedTuple keyed by lowercased experiment name.\n\nexperiments = configure_experiments([\"deepcore\", \"dayabay\"])\n# (deepcore = ..., dayabay = ...)\n\n\n\nconfigure_experiments(experiment_list, physics)\n\nLike the single-argument form but passes a shared physics object to each experiment's configure(physics) method, allowing a custom oscillation or cross-section configuration to be shared across all experiments.\n\nexperiments = configure_experiments([\"deepcore\", \"dayabay\"], physics)\n\n\n\nsave_result(result, name)\n\nSave a NewtrinosResult to <name>.jld2 in the current working directory using JLD2.\n\nsave_result(result, \"myrun\")   # writes myrun.jld2\n\n\n\nplot_result(result, name, vars_to_scan; title=nothing)\n\nRender a scan or profile result and save it as <name>.png. Requires a Makie backend (e.g. using CairoMakie) to be loaded in the caller's scope before calling this function. The x-axis is labelled with the first scanned parameter; the y-axis shows -2ΔLLH (1-D) or the second scanned parameter (2-D).\n\nusing CairoMakie\nplot_result(result, \"myrun\", vars_to_scan; title=\"DeepCore scan\")","title":"Helper Functions (cli_common.jl)"},{"category":"section","location":"api/analysis/#Examples","page":"Analysis","text":"# 2-D scan over θ₂₃ and Δm²₃₁ with DeepCore + Daya Bay, save PNG\njulia --project src/analysis/analysis.jl \\\n  --experiments deepcore dayabay \\\n  --name joint_scan \\\n  --task scan \\\n  --plot\n\n# Profile likelihood using 4 distributed workers, 2 threads each\njulia --project src/analysis/distributed_profile.jl \\\n  --experiments deepcore \\\n  --name dc_profile \\\n  --workers 4 \\\n  --threads 2\n\n# Importance sampling posterior\njulia --project src/analysis/analysis.jl \\\n  --experiments deepcore \\\n  --name dc_posterior \\\n  --task importancesampling","title":"Examples"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.get_params","page":"Analysis","text":"get_params(x) -> NamedTuple\n\nExtract nominal parameter values as a sorted NamedTuple.\n\nDispatches on the type of x:\n\nPhysics: returns x.params sorted by key.\nExperiment: merges x.params with the parameters of its physics module.\nWrapper: applies the alias translation from Wrapper to the keys.\nNamedTuple of modules: merges parameters from all modules via safe_merge, raising an error on conflicts.\n\nArguments\n\nx: a Newtrinos.Physics, Newtrinos.Experiment, Wrapper, or NamedTuple of such objects.\n\nReturns\n\nA sorted NamedTuple of parameter name → nominal value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.get_params"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.get_priors","page":"Analysis","text":"get_priors(x) -> NamedTuple\n\nExtract prior distributions as a sorted NamedTuple.\n\nDispatches on the type of x:\n\nPhysics: returns x.priors sorted by key.\nExperiment: merges x.priors with priors from its physics module.\nWrapper: applies the alias translation from Wrapper to the keys.\nNamedTuple of modules: merges priors from all modules via safe_merge, raising an error on conflicts.\n\nArguments\n\nx: a Newtrinos.Physics, Newtrinos.Experiment, Wrapper, or NamedTuple of such objects.\n\nReturns\n\nA sorted NamedTuple of parameter name → prior distribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.get_priors"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.condition","page":"Analysis","text":"condition(priors, conditional_vars, params) -> NamedTuple\n\nFix a subset of parameters to specific values by replacing their prior distributions with constants (ConstValueDist).\n\nTwo dispatch methods:\n\nArray form (conditionalvars is an array) — fixes each symbol in `conditionalvarsto its value inparams`:\n\ncondition(priors, [:theta23, :dm31_sq], params)\n\nDict form (conditional_vars is a dict) — fixes each key to the paired value, or to params[key] when the value is nothing:\n\ncondition(priors, Dict(:theta23 => pi/4, :dm31_sq => nothing), params)\n\nArguments\n\npriors::NamedTuple: prior distributions to modify.\nconditional_vars: AbstractArray{Symbol} or AbstractDict{Symbol} of parameters to fix.\nparams::NamedTuple: fallback parameter values used when a dict entry is nothing or when using the array form.\n\nReturns\n\nThe modified priors NamedTuple with the specified parameters frozen.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.condition"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.safe_merge","page":"Analysis","text":"safe_merge(nt_list::NamedTuple...) -> NamedTuple\n\nMerge NamedTuples, asserting that any key shared by two or more inputs has the same value in all of them.\n\nRaises an error if a key conflict is detected. The result is sorted alphabetically by key.\n\nArguments\n\nnt_list::NamedTuple...: one or more NamedTuples to merge.\n\nReturns\n\nA sorted NamedTuple containing all key-value pairs from the inputs.\n\nExamples\n\na = (x = 1.0, y = 2.0)\nb = (y = 2.0, z = 3.0)\nsafe_merge(a, b)   # (x = 1.0, y = 2.0, z = 3.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.safe_merge"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.correlated_priors_vars","page":"Analysis","text":"correlated_priors_vars(priors, vars, dist) -> (corr_prior, other_prior)\n\nReplace the independent priors for vars with a single correlated multivariate distribution, splitting the prior NamedTuple into two parts.\n\nUseful when a set of parameters has a known covariance structure (e.g. from an external fit) that cannot be captured by independent marginals.\n\nArguments\n\npriors::NamedTuple: the full prior NamedTuple.\nvars::Union{AbstractArray, Tuple}: parameter names to correlate.\ndist::Distributions.Distribution: a multivariate distribution over vars (must match the length of vars).\n\nReturns\n\nA 2-tuple (corr_prior, other_prior) where:\n\ncorr_prior: a callable returning a ReshapedDist over vars.\nother_prior: a distprod of the remaining (uncorrelated) priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.correlated_priors_vars"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.generate_asimov_data","page":"Analysis","text":"generate_asimov_data(experiment, params) -> data\ngenerate_asimov_data(experiments::NamedTuple, params) -> NamedTuple\n\nGenerate Asimov (expected-value) data from the experiment forward model.\n\nEvaluates mean(forward_model(params)). For Poisson-distributed observables the mean is rounded to the nearest integer so that the data type matches what a real run would produce.\n\nArguments\n\nexperiment: a single Newtrinos.Experiment or a NamedTuple of them.\nparams::NamedTuple: parameter values at which to evaluate the forward model.\n\nReturns\n\nSingle-experiment form: the expected-value data array (or NamedTuple of arrays).\nMulti-experiment form: a NamedTuple with the same keys as experiments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.generate_asimov_data"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.generate_toy_data","page":"Analysis","text":"generate_toy_data(experiment, params) -> data\ngenerate_toy_data(experiments::NamedTuple, params) -> NamedTuple\n\nGenerate random toy data by sampling from the experiment forward model.\n\nCalls experiment.forward_model(params) to obtain the predictive distribution and draws one sample from it.\n\nArguments\n\nexperiment: a single Newtrinos.Experiment or a NamedTuple of them.\nparams::NamedTuple: parameter values at which to evaluate the forward model.\n\nReturns\n\nSingle-experiment form: one sample from the forward model distribution.\nMulti-experiment form: a NamedTuple with the same keys as experiments, each entry holding one sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.generate_toy_data"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.make_prior_samples","page":"Analysis","text":"make_prior_samples(posterior, nsamples::Int=10_000) -> NamedTuple\n\nInitialize importance samples by using a standard normal as the proposal distribution.\n\nTransforms posterior to the standard-normal space via BAT's PriorToNormal reparameterization, then generates nsamples samples by calling importance_sampling  with the transformed posterior and the standard normal mathcalN(0 I) as the proposal distribution. \n\nArguments\n\nposterior: a BAT posterior measure.\nnsamples::Int: number of importance samples to draw (default: 10_000).\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple with fields:\n\napprox_dist: the standard normal proposal used.\nsamples_p: DensitySampleVector in the transformed (normal) space.\nsamples_user: samples back-transformed to the original parameter space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.make_prior_samples"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.make_init_samples","page":"Analysis","text":"make_init_samples(posterior, nseeds::Int=10, nsamples::Int=10_000) -> NamedTuple\n\nInitialize importance samples by fitting a mixture of local Gaussian approximations at posterior modes.\n\nFinds nseeds approximate modes via L-BFGS optimization (using Sobol-sequence starting points), fits a local_MGVI_approx at each mode, and combines them into a MixtureModel. The mixture model weights are calculated as \n\nlog tildew_i = log p (mu_i) - log q (mu_i) \ntildew_i = expbigl(log tildew_i - max(log tildew)bigr) \nw_i = fractildew_isum_j tildew_j \n\nwhere mu_i is the mode of the i-th component. Importance samples are then drawn from the mixture via importance_sampling.\n\nArguments\n\nposterior: a BAT posterior measure.\nnseeds::Int: number of mode-finding restarts (default: 10).\nnsamples::Int: number of importance samples to draw (default: 10_000).\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple with fields:\n\napprox_dist::MixtureModel: the weighted Gaussian mixture proposal.\nsamples_p: DensitySampleVector in the transformed space.\nsamples_user: samples back-transformed to the original parameter space.\n\n\n\n\n\nmake_init_samples(posterior, seed_points::DataFrame, nsamples::Int=10_000) -> NamedTuple\n\nInitialize importance samples from user-supplied seed points given as a DataFrame.\n\nLike the integer-seed form but uses rows of seed_points as starting points for mode-finding instead of Sobol samples. Each row is projected into the transformed parameter space and L-BFGS is run from there.\n\nwarning: Warning\nThis method contains hardcoded field names (Darkdim_radius, ca1, ca2, ca3) specific to Darkdim models. It must be generalized before use with other parameter sets.\n\nArguments\n\nposterior: a BAT posterior measure.\nseed_points::DataFrame: each row provides the starting parameter values. Must contain columns Darkdim_radius, ca1, ca2, ca3.\nnsamples::Int: number of importance samples to draw (default: 10_000).\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple with fields:\n\napprox_dist::MixtureModel: the weighted Gaussian mixture proposal.\nsamples_p: DensitySampleVector in the transformed space.\nsamples_user: samples back-transformed to the original parameter space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.make_init_samples"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.whack_a_mole","page":"Analysis","text":"whack_a_mole(posterior, init_samples, n_whack=100) -> NamedTuple\n\nRefine an importance sampling approximation by iteratively adding Gaussian components at high-weight sample points.\n\nAt each of n_whack iterations:\n\nThe highest-weight sample in the current mixture is identified.\nA local_MGVI_approx is fitted at that point.\nThe new component is added to the mixture with weight proportional to its posterior density at its mode relative to the mixture density.\nNew samples are drawn from the new component and importance-reweighted.\n\nThis \"whack-a-mole\" strategy adaptively targets undersampled regions of the posterior. Use whack_many_moles for a parallelized variant with convergence criteria.\n\nArguments\n\nposterior: a BAT posterior measure.\ninit_samples: initialization NamedTuple as returned by make_init_samples or make_prior_samples.\nn_whack::Int: number of refinement iterations (default: 100).\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple with fields:\n\napprox_dist::MixtureModel: the refined Gaussian mixture proposal.\nsamples_p: accumulated DensitySampleVector in the transformed space.\nsamples_user: samples back-transformed to the original parameter space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.whack_a_mole"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.whack_many_moles","page":"Analysis","text":"whack_many_moles(posterior, init_samples;\n                 target_efficiency=Inf, target_samplesize=Inf,\n                 maxiter=100, n_parallel=Threads.nthreads(),\n                 cache_dir=nothing) -> NamedTuple\n\nParallelized adaptive importance sampling with convergence criteria.\n\nLike whack_a_mole but processes n_parallel high-weight sample points simultaneously per each iteration using Threads.@threads. Iteration stops when any of the following conditions is met:\n\nEffective sample size (ESS) exceeds target_samplesize.\nEfficiency (ESS / total samples) exceeds target_efficiency.\nNumber of iterations reaches maxiter.\n\nIntermediate results can be saved to disk after each iteration for fault tolerance.\n\nArguments\n\nposterior: a BAT posterior measure.\ninit_samples: initialization NamedTuple as returned by make_init_samples or make_prior_samples.\ntarget_efficiency::Real: stop when ESS / n_samples exceeds this value (default: Inf, i.e. no efficiency target).\ntarget_samplesize::Real: stop when ESS exceeds this value (default: Inf, i.e. no ESS target).\nmaxiter::Int: maximum number of refinement iterations (default: 100).\nn_parallel::Int: number of new components to fit per iteration (default: Threads.nthreads()).\ncache_dir::Union{String,Nothing}: if provided, saves a JLD2 checkpoint after each iteration to this directory. (Default: nothing).\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple with fields:\n\napprox_dist::MixtureModel: the refined Gaussian mixture proposal.\nsamples_p: accumulated DensitySampleVector in the transformed space.\nsamples_user: samples back-transformed to the original parameter space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.whack_many_moles"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.importance_sampling","page":"Analysis","text":"importance_sampling(pstr, approx_dist, nsamples) -> DensitySampleVector\n\nDraw importance-weighted samples from pstr using approx_dist as the proposal.\n\nSamples nsamples points from approx_dist, evaluates log p (pstr) and log q (approx_dist) at each point, and computes normalized importance weights. The evaluation of log p is parallelized over threads. The weights are calculated as:\n\nlog tildew_i = log p - log q\nw_i = expbigl( log tildew_i - max(log tildew_i) bigr)\n\nwith the max(log tildew_i) substracted in the exponent such that  max_i w_i = 1 for numerical stability. \n\nArguments\n\npstr: BAT posterior (typically already transformed via PriorToNormal).\napprox_dist: proposal distribution supporting bat_sample and logdensityof.\nnsamples::Int: number of samples to draw from approx_dist.\n\nReturns\n\nA DensitySampleVector with samples from approx_dist, their respective log p, and their importance weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.importance_sampling"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.local_MGVI_approx","page":"Analysis","text":"local_MGVI_approx(pstr, θ_sel) -> MvNormal\n\nFit a local Gaussian approximation to pstr at the selected point θ_sel.\n\nUses the MGVI (Metric Gaussian Variational Inference) approach: the covariance is\n\nSigma = bigl(J^top F(theta) J + Ibigr)^-1\n\nwhere F(theta) is the Fisher information matrix of the likelihood model evaluated at theta, and J = partial_theta(textttflat_params circ m) is the Jacobian of the parameter reparameterization. The result is regularized with a Cholesky decomposition via PositiveFactorizations.\n\nArguments\n\npstr: BAT posterior with a likelihood field exposing the forward model k.\nθ_sel: parameter vector (in the transformed space) at which to centre the Gaussian.\n\nReturns\n\nA MvNormal distribution centred at θ_sel with covariance Sigma.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.local_MGVI_approx"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.generate_likelihood","page":"Analysis","text":"generate_likelihood(experiments[, observed]) -> likelihood\n\nConstruct a joint likelihood from a NamedTuple of configured experiments.\n\nCombines all experiment forward models into a product distribution and pairs it with the observed data. The returned object supports DensityInterface.logdensityof(likelihood, params).\n\nArguments\n\nexperiments::NamedTuple: named collection of configured Newtrinos.Experiment objects.\nobserved (optional): NamedTuple of observed data arrays matching experiments. Defaults to the observed data stored in each experiment's assets.observed field.\n\nReturns\n\nA likelihood object compatible with DensityInterface.logdensityof.\n\nExamples\n\nexperiments = (deepcore = Newtrinos.deepcore.configure(),\n               dayabay  = Newtrinos.dayabay.configure())\nllh     = generate_likelihood(experiments)\nlogdensityof(llh, params)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.generate_likelihood"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.get_observed","page":"Analysis","text":"get_observed(experiments::NamedTuple) -> NamedTuple\n\nExtract the observed data from each experiment in a NamedTuple of experiments.\n\nArguments\n\nexperiments::NamedTuple: named collection of Newtrinos.Experiment objects.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple with the same keys as experiments, each entry holding the corresponding experiment.assets.observed data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.get_observed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.get_fwd_model","page":"Analysis","text":"get_fwd_model(experiments::NamedTuple) -> Function\n\nCompose the forward models of all experiments into a single joint model.\n\nBuilds a NamedTuple of per-experiment forward models and composes them with ffanout and distprod so that calling the result with a parameter NamedTuple returns a joint product distribution over all experiments.\n\nArguments\n\nexperiments::NamedTuple: named collection of Newtrinos.Experiment objects.\n\nReturns\n\nA callable params -> joint_distribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.get_fwd_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.find_mle","page":"Analysis","text":"find_mle(likelihood, prior, params; adsel=AutoPolyesterForwardDiff())\n    -> (llh, log_posterior, result)\n\nFind the Maximum Likelihood Estimator (MLE) using L-BFGS optimization via BAT.jl.\n\nParameters whose prior is a ConstValueDist are held fixed during optimization. If the optimizer raises an ArgumentError (e.g. due to a degenerate starting point), returns (NaN, NaN, NaN-filled NamedTuple) instead of propagating the error.\n\nArguments\n\nlikelihood: a likelihood object supporting DensityInterface.logdensityof.\nprior: a distprod-style prior NamedTuple or distribution.\nparams::NamedTuple: starting parameter values for the optimizer.\nadsel: AD backend selector (default: AutoPolyesterForwardDiff()).\n\nReturns\n\nA 3-tuple (llh, log_posterior, result) where:\n\nllh::Float64: log-likelihood at the optimum.\nlog_posterior::Float64: log-posterior at the optimum.\nresult::NamedTuple: optimized parameter values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.find_mle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.find_mle_cached","page":"Analysis","text":"find_mle_cached(likelihood, prior, params, cache_dir)\n    -> (llh, log_posterior, result)\n\nLike find_mle but caches results to disk using content hashing.\n\nA hash of (prior, params) is computed with ContentHashes.hash; if a matching .jld2 file exists in cache_dir the optimization is skipped and the cached result is returned. Otherwise the result is computed and saved.\n\nArguments\n\nlikelihood: a likelihood object supporting DensityInterface.logdensityof.\nprior: prior distribution (same as find_mle).\nparams::NamedTuple: starting parameter values.\ncache_dir::Union{String,Nothing}: directory for cached .jld2 files. Pass nothing to disable caching.\n\nReturns\n\nA 3-tuple (llh, log_posterior, result) — see find_mle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.find_mle_cached"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.profile","page":"Analysis","text":"profile(likelihood, priors, vars_to_scan, params;\n        cache_dir=nothing, map_func=nothing) -> NewtrinosResult\n\nRun a profile likelihood scan over a parameter grid.\n\nCreates grid points defined by vars_to_scan with generate_scanpoints. At each grid point, all nuisance parameters are optimized with _profile , i.e. via find_mle_cached.  If all non-scanned parameters have fixed (Number) priors, falls back to scan automatically. Also collects meta data of the profile process and attaches it the returned object.\n\nArguments\n\nlikelihood: a likelihood object supporting DensityInterface.logdensityof.\npriors::NamedTuple: prior distributions for all parameters.\nvars_to_scan::OrderedDict{Symbol,Int}: parameters to scan and grid sizes.\nparams::NamedTuple: starting values for nuisance parameter optimization.\ncache_dir::Union{String,Nothing}: directory for caching MLE results (created if absent). Pass nothing to disable (this is the default).\nmap_func: custom mapping function for parallelism (e.g. pmap for distributed workers). Defaults to Threads.@threads.\n\nReturns\n\nA NewtrinosResult NewtrinosResult(axes, values, meta)  with the scan grid axes, per-point profiling results, and meta data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.profile"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.scan","page":"Analysis","text":"scan(likelihood, priors, vars_to_scan, params;\n     gradient_map=false) -> NewtrinosResult\n\nRun a simple likelihood scan on a parameter grid (NO nuisance optimization).\n\nGenerates grid with _generate_grid via vars_to_scan and then  evaluates logdensityof(likelihood, params) at each grid point without optimizing over nuisance parameters. Faster than profile but does not account for nuisance parameter variations.  Also collects meta data of the scan process and attaches it the returned object.\n\nArguments\n\nlikelihood: a likelihood object supporting DensityInterface.logdensityof.\npriors::NamedTuple: prior distributions used to place grid points at quantiles.\nvars_to_scan::OrderedDict{Symbol,Int}: parameters to scan and grid sizes.\nparams::NamedTuple: nominal values; non-scanned parameters are held fixed.\ngradient_map::Bool: default=false; if true, also evaluates ForwardDiff.gradient at each grid point and includes per-parameter gradient arrays in the result.\n\nReturns\n\nA NewtrinosResult NewtrinosResult(axes, values, meta)  with the scan grid axes, per-point scan results, and meta data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.scan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.bestfit","page":"Analysis","text":"bestfit(result::NewtrinosResult) -> NamedTuple\n\nExtract the best-fit parameter values from a scan or profile result.\n\nFinds the grid point with the highest log_posterior and returns both the scanned parameter values (from result.axes) and all optimized nuisance parameter values at that point.\n\nArguments\n\nresult::NewtrinosResult: output of scan or profile.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple containing all parameter values at the best-fit point, including the scanned axis values, nuisance parameters, llh, and log_posterior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.bestfit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.generate_scanpoints","page":"Analysis","text":"generate_scanpoints(vars_to_scan, priors) -> (values, scanpoints)\n\nBuild a grid of fixed-parameter priors for a profile likelihood scan.\n\nFor each variable in vars_to_scan, grid points are placed at evenly-spaced quantiles of its prior distribution. All combinations are formed via a Cartesian product. At each grid point, the prior for the scanned variables is replaced with a ConstValueDist fixing them to the grid values.\n\nArguments\n\nvars_to_scan::OrderedDict{Symbol,Int}: maps each parameter name to the desired number of evenly spaced grid points within the prior range.\npriors::NamedTuple: prior distributions for all parameters.\n\nReturns\n\nA 2-tuple (values, scanpoints) where:\n\nvalues: vector of grid-point vectors (one per scanned parameter).\nscanpoints: array of distprod priors, one per grid point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.generate_scanpoints"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.assemble_profile_results","page":"Analysis","text":"assemble_profile_results(opt_results, result_size) -> NamedTuple\n\nCollect the 'raw' per-grid-point optimization results and structure them into flat arrays within a NamedTuple.\n\nStacks the (llh, log_posterior, params) 3-tuples returned by find_mle_cached into a NamedTuple of arrays with shape result_size.\n\nArguments\n\nopt_results: iterable of (llh, log_posterior, NamedTuple) 3-tuples.\nresult_size: shape of the output arrays (matching the scan grid).\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple with one array-entry per parameter (values across the grid) plus :llh and :log_posterior arrays.\n\nExample\n\nopt_results = [\n    (-10.0, -12.0, (a=1.0, b=2.0)), # llh, log_posterior, params output of find_mle\n    (-5.0,  -7.0,  (a=3.0, b=4.0))\n]\nres = Newtrinos.assemble_profile_results(opt_results, (2,))\n# returns res = (a = [1.0, 3.0], b = [2.0, 4.0], llh = [-10.0, -5.0], log_posterior = [-12.0, -7.0])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.assemble_profile_results"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.add_meta!","page":"Analysis","text":"add_meta!(meta::Dict)\n\nPopulate a metadata dictionary with execution environment information.\n\nAdds the following keys in-place:\n\n\"hostname\": result of gethostname().\n\"username\": from ENV[\"USER\"] or ENV[\"USERNAME\"].\n\"date\": current date-time formatted as \"yyyy-mm-dd HH:MM:SS\".\n\"repo\": path to the Newtrinos.jl repository root.\n\"commit_hash\": current HEAD commit hash.\n\"repo_clean\": true if the repository has no uncommitted changes.\n\nCalled automatically by profile and scan before returning their NewtrinosResult. \n\nArguments\n\nmeta::Dict: dictionary to populate in-place.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing (mutates meta).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.add_meta!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos.sort_nt","page":"Analysis","text":"sort_nt(nt::NamedTuple) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturn a copy of a named tuple nt with keys sorted alphabetically.\n\nUsed internally by safe_merge, get_params, and get_priors to ensure a canonical key ordering across modules.\n\nArguments\n\nnt::NamedTuple: input NamedTuple.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple with the same entries as nt, sorted alphabetically by key name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.sort_nt"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos._generate_grid","page":"Analysis","text":"_generate_grid(vars_to_scan, priors) -> (vars, values, mesh)\n\nConstruct the Cartesian grid used by generate_scanpoints and scan.\n\nGrid points for each variable are placed at evenly-spaced quantiles of its prior distribution. All combinations are formed via IterTools.product.\n\nArguments\n\nvars_to_scan::OrderedDict{Symbol,Int}: maps parameter names to grid sizes.\npriors::NamedTuple: prior distributions used to compute quantiles.\n\nReturns\n\nA 3-tuple (vars, values, mesh) where:\n\nvars: Vector{Symbol} of scanned parameter names.\nvalues: vector of grid-point vectors (one per variable).\nmesh: Cartesian product array of grid-point tuples.\n\nExamples\n\n    priors = (a=Uniform(0.0, 2.0), b=Uniform(-1.0, 1.0))\n    vars_to_scan = OrderedDict(:a => 3, :b => 4)\n    vars, values, mesh = Newtrinos._generate_grid(vars_to_scan, priors)\n    # => mesh is a 3x4 grid of evenly spaced points from within the prior range of a and b \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos._generate_grid"},{"category":"function","location":"api/analysis/#Newtrinos._profile","page":"Analysis","text":"_profile(likelihood, scanpoints, params, cache_dir; map_func=nothing)\n    -> NamedTuple\n\nExecute find_mle_cached at every point in scanpoints, collect the results, and assemble them into a NamedTuple of result arrays with assemble_profile_results.\n\nUses Threads.@threads by default. Pass a custom map_func (e.g. pmap) to override the parallelism strategy.\n\nArguments\n\nlikelihood: a likelihood object supporting DensityInterface.logdensityof.\nscanpoints: array of prior objects, one per grid point.\nparams::NamedTuple: starting values for nuisance optimization.\ncache_dir::Union{String,Nothing}: passed to find_mle_cached.\nmap_func: optional custom mapping function; default=nothing uses threaded loops.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple of result arrays as produced by assemble_profile_results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos._profile"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/custom_physics/#Custom-Physics","page":"Custom Physics","text":"By default, each experiment configures its own physics modules. You can override this to use custom oscillation models, inverted ordering, or BSM physics.","title":"Custom Physics"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/custom_physics/#Custom-Oscillation-Configuration","page":"Custom Physics","text":"using Newtrinos\n\nosc_cfg = Newtrinos.osc.OscillationConfig(\n    flavour     = Newtrinos.osc.ThreeFlavour(ordering=:IO),\n    propagation = Newtrinos.osc.Basic(),\n    states      = Newtrinos.osc.All(),\n    interaction = Newtrinos.osc.SI(),\n)\n\nosc = Newtrinos.osc.configure(osc_cfg)","title":"Custom Oscillation Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/custom_physics/#Assembling-a-Physics-Bundle","page":"Custom Physics","text":"Atmospheric experiments need oscillations, fluxes, Earth density, and cross-sections:\n\natm_flux     = Newtrinos.atm_flux.configure()\nearth_layers = Newtrinos.earth_layers.configure()\nxsec         = Newtrinos.xsec.configure()\n\nphysics = (; osc, atm_flux, earth_layers, xsec)","title":"Assembling a Physics Bundle"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/custom_physics/#Passing-Physics-to-Experiments","page":"Custom Physics","text":"experiments = (\n    deepcore = Newtrinos.deepcore.configure(physics),\n    dayabay  = Newtrinos.dayabay.configure(physics),\n)\n\nEach experiment extracts whatever physics modules it needs. Reactor experiments (Daya Bay, KamLAND) only use osc; atmospheric experiments use all four.","title":"Passing Physics to Experiments"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/custom_physics/#Available-Flavour-Models","page":"Custom Physics","text":"ThreeFlavour(ordering=:NO) / ThreeFlavour(ordering=:IO) — standard 3-flavour\nSterile() — 3+1 sterile neutrino model\nADD() — large extra dimensions\nDarkdim_tower(), Darkdim_lightest() — dark dimension models","title":"Available Flavour Models"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/custom_physics/#Available-Interaction-Models","page":"Custom Physics","text":"Vacuum() — vacuum oscillations only\nSI() — standard matter effects (MSW)\nNSI() — non-standard interactions","title":"Available Interaction Models"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/custom_physics/#Available-Propagation-Models","page":"Custom Physics","text":"Basic() — standard propagation\nDecoherent() — quantum decoherence\nDamping() — damped oscillations","title":"Available Propagation Models"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_experiment/#Single-Experiment","page":"Single Experiment","text":"This tutorial shows how to configure a single experiment and explore its likelihood.","title":"Single Experiment"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_experiment/#Configuring-an-Experiment","page":"Single Experiment","text":"Each experiment has a configure() function that returns a struct with everything needed for analysis. Using defaults:\n\nusing Newtrinos\nusing DensityInterface\n\nexp = Newtrinos.dayabay.configure()\n\nThe returned struct contains:\n\nphysics — configured physics modules (oscillations, etc.)\nparams — nominal parameter values (NamedTuple)\npriors — prior distributions (NamedTuple)\nassets — data, MC, interpolations, etc.\nforward_model — callable that maps parameters to a predicted distribution\nplot — visualization function","title":"Configuring an Experiment"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_experiment/#Extracting-Parameters-and-Priors","page":"Single Experiment","text":"Wrap the experiment in a NamedTuple and use the accessor functions:\n\nexperiments = (; dayabay = exp)\nparams = Newtrinos.get_params(experiments)\npriors = Newtrinos.get_priors(experiments)\n\nget_params and get_priors recursively merge parameters from both the experiment and its physics modules, checking for conflicts.","title":"Extracting Parameters and Priors"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_experiment/#Evaluating-the-Likelihood","page":"Single Experiment","text":"likelihood = Newtrinos.generate_likelihood(experiments)\nllh = logdensityof(likelihood, params)","title":"Evaluating the Likelihood"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_experiment/#Computing-Gradients","page":"Single Experiment","text":"All code is ForwardDiff-compatible:\n\nusing ForwardDiff\n\nf(p) = logdensityof(likelihood, p)\ngrad = ForwardDiff.gradient(f, params)","title":"Computing Gradients"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_experiment/#Plotting","page":"Single Experiment","text":"If the experiment provides a plot function:\n\nimg = exp.plot(params)","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/single_experiment/#Running-a-Scan","page":"Single Experiment","text":"using DataStructures\n\n# Fix some parameters, scan over others\nconditional_vars = Dict(:θ₁₂ => params.θ₁₂)\npriors = Newtrinos.condition(priors, conditional_vars, params)\n\nvars_to_scan = OrderedDict(:θ₁₃ => 21)\nresult = Newtrinos.scan(likelihood, priors, vars_to_scan, params)","title":"Running a Scan"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/experiments/#Experiments","page":"Experiments","text":"Newtrinos.jl includes likelihoods for 11 neutrino experiments across four categories.","title":"Experiments"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/experiments/#Atmospheric-Neutrinos","page":"Experiments","text":"These experiments require oscillation physics with matter effects (SI), atmospheric fluxes, Earth density profiles, and cross-sections.\n\nExperiment Module Description\nIceCube DeepCore Newtrinos.deepcore 9-year verification sample\nSuper-Kamiokande Newtrinos.super_k Atmospheric 2023 analysis\nORCA Newtrinos.orca KM3NeT/ORCA 6-line, 433 kton-years\nIceCube Upgrade Newtrinos.ic_upgrade Simulated upgrade detector","title":"Atmospheric Neutrinos"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/experiments/#Reactor-Neutrinos","page":"Experiments","text":"Reactor experiments use vacuum oscillations only.\n\nExperiment Module Description\nDaya Bay Newtrinos.dayabay 3158-day dataset\nKamLAND Newtrinos.kamland 7-year dataset\nJUNO Newtrinos.juno Simulated (no observed data)\nTAO Newtrinos.tao Simulated (no observed data)","title":"Reactor Neutrinos"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/experiments/#Accelerator-Neutrinos","page":"Experiments","text":"Experiment Module Description\nMINOS Newtrinos.minos Sterile search, 16e20 POT","title":"Accelerator Neutrinos"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/experiments/#Coherent-Elastic-Neutrino-Nucleus-Scattering","page":"Experiments","text":"Self-contained experiments with their own flux and cross-section models.\n\nExperiment Module Description\nCOHERENT CsI Newtrinos.coherent_csi CsI detector\nCOHERENT LAr Newtrinos.coherent_lAr Liquid Argon detector","title":"Coherent Elastic Neutrino-Nucleus Scattering"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/experiments/#Usage","page":"Experiments","text":"Each experiment is configured via its configure() function:\n\n# With default physics\nexp = Newtrinos.dayabay.configure()\n\n# With custom physics\nexp = Newtrinos.deepcore.configure(physics)\n\nEach experiment's subdirectory under src/experiments/ contains a test.jl script that reproduces official results.","title":"Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/joint_analysis/#Joint-Analysis","page":"Joint Analysis","text":"This tutorial shows how to combine multiple experiments into a joint likelihood and run profile/scan analyses.","title":"Joint Analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/joint_analysis/#Combining-Experiments","page":"Joint Analysis","text":"using Newtrinos\nusing DensityInterface\nusing DataStructures\n\nexperiments = (\n    deepcore = Newtrinos.deepcore.configure(),\n    dayabay  = Newtrinos.dayabay.configure(),\n    kamland  = Newtrinos.kamland.configure(),\n    minos    = Newtrinos.minos.configure(),\n)\n\nParameters and priors are automatically merged across all experiments and their physics modules:\n\nparams = Newtrinos.get_params(experiments)\npriors = Newtrinos.get_priors(experiments)\nlikelihood = Newtrinos.generate_likelihood(experiments)","title":"Combining Experiments"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/joint_analysis/#Conditioning-(Fixing-Parameters)","page":"Joint Analysis","text":"Fix parameters to specific values before scanning:\n\nconditional_vars = Dict(\n    :θ₁₂  => params.θ₁₂,\n    :δCP   => -1.89,\n    :Δm²₂₁ => params.Δm²₂₁,\n)\npriors = Newtrinos.condition(priors, conditional_vars, params)","title":"Conditioning (Fixing Parameters)"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/joint_analysis/#Likelihood-Scan","page":"Joint Analysis","text":"A scan evaluates the likelihood on a grid without optimization:\n\nusing Distributions, Accessors\n\n@reset priors.θ₂₃ = Uniform(pi/4 - 0.2, pi/4 + 0.2)\n@reset priors.Δm²₃₁ = Uniform(0.0018, 0.0028)\n\nvars_to_scan = OrderedDict(:θ₂₃ => 31, :Δm²₃₁ => 31)\nresult = Newtrinos.scan(likelihood, priors, vars_to_scan, params)","title":"Likelihood Scan"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/joint_analysis/#Profile-Likelihood","page":"Joint Analysis","text":"A profile scan optimizes over nuisance parameters at each grid point:\n\nvars_to_scan = OrderedDict(:θ₂₃ => 11, :Δm²₃₁ => 11)\nresult = Newtrinos.profile(likelihood, priors, vars_to_scan, params; cache_dir=\"my_profile\")\n\nResults are cached to disk, so interrupted runs can be resumed.","title":"Profile Likelihood"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/joint_analysis/#Plotting-Results","page":"Joint Analysis","text":"using CairoMakie\n\nfig = Figure()\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel=\"θ₂₃\", ylabel=\"Δm²₃₁\")\nplot!(ax, result)\nsave(\"contours.png\", fig)","title":"Plotting Results"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/joint_analysis/#Extracting-Best-Fit","page":"Joint Analysis","text":"bf = Newtrinos.bestfit(result)","title":"Extracting Best Fit"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Types","page":"Types","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Module","page":"Types","text":"","title":"Module"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Core-Abstract-Types","page":"Types","text":"","title":"Core Abstract Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Result-Type","page":"Types","text":"","title":"Result Type"},{"category":"section","location":"api/types/#Wrapper-Type","page":"Types","text":"","title":"Wrapper Type"},{"category":"module","location":"api/types/#Newtrinos.Newtrinos","page":"Types","text":"Newtrinos\n\nA Julia package for global analysis of neutrino data. Provides modular physics models, experimental likelihoods, and inference tools (profile likelihood, Bayesian sampling).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.Newtrinos"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#Newtrinos.Physics","page":"Types","text":"Physics\n\nAbstract type for physics modules. Subtypes must have params::NamedTuple and priors::NamedTuple fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.Physics"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#Newtrinos.Experiment","page":"Types","text":"Experiment\n\nAbstract type for experiment modules. Subtypes must have physics, params, priors, assets, forward_model, and plot fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.Experiment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#Newtrinos.NewtrinosResult","page":"Types","text":"NewtrinosResult(; axes, values, meta=Dict())\n\nContainer for the results of a scan or profile run.\n\nFields\n\naxes::NamedTuple: scan grid coordinates, one entry per scanned parameter. Each entry is a vector of grid-point values.\nvalues::NamedTuple: per-grid-point outputs — likelihood values (llh, log_posterior) and optimized nuisance parameter arrays.\nmeta::Dict: execution metadata (hostname, date, git commit, timing). Populated by add_meta!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.NewtrinosResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/types/#Newtrinos.Wrapper","page":"Types","text":"Wrapper <: Newtrinos.Experiment\n\nWraps a Newtrinos.Experiment with parameter name aliases.\n\nWhen two experiments share a physics parameter under different names (e.g. theta13 vs sin2theta13), a Wrapper renames parameters seen by the outer analysis while transparently translating back to the original names before calling the inner experiment's forward_model and plot functions.\n\nConstruct with Wrapper(experiment, aliases) where aliases is a Dict{Symbol,Symbol} mapping original parameter names to their aliases.\n\nFields\n\nx::Newtrinos.Experiment: the wrapped experiment.\naliases::Dict{Symbol,Symbol}: map from original name → alias.\ntranslated_keys::Vector{Symbol}: all parameter keys after alias substitution.\nreverse_lookup::Dict{Symbol,Symbol}: map from alias → original name.\n\nExamples\n\naliases = Dict(:dm21_sq => :Dm21)\nwrapped = Wrapper(dayabay_experiment, aliases)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.Wrapper"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/performance/#Performance","page":"Performance","text":"","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/performance/#Hot-Path:-Oscillation-Probabilities","page":"Performance","text":"osc_prob is the performance-critical function. For 3-flavour vacuum oscillations, it uses SMatrix/SVector from StaticArrays.jl for zero-allocation computation. Matter effects require eigendecomposition which allocates (~19 allocs/call).","title":"Hot Path: Oscillation Probabilities"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/performance/#ForwardDiff-Chunk-Size","page":"Performance","text":"ForwardDiff processes parameters in chunks of 12 by default. With N parameters, gradient computation costs ceil(N/12) forward passes. For example, 21 parameters (typical for DeepCore) requires 2 passes.","title":"ForwardDiff Chunk Size"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/performance/#Response-Matrix-Contractions","page":"Performance","text":"Super-Kamiokande uses contract_R with pre-flattened Float64 matrices for BLAS-accelerated matrix-vector multiply, avoiding Dual number broadcast over large arrays.","title":"Response Matrix Contractions"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/performance/#Benchmarking","page":"Performance","text":"Run the likelihood benchmark:\n\njulia -t 4 --project benchmark/bench_likelihood.jl --experiments deepcore dayabay\n\nThis benchmarks both likelihood evaluation and gradient computation, comparing ForwardDiff and PolyesterForwardDiff (threaded chunked differentiation).\n\nRun the oscillation benchmark:\n\njulia --project benchmark/bench_osc.jl","title":"Benchmarking"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/performance/#Tips","page":"Performance","text":"Use julia -t N to enable threading for scan/profile tasks\nFor large grids, use --workers N for distributed parallelism\nProfile caching (cache_dir) prevents redundant MLE computations when re-running\nPolyesterForwardDiff provides ~1.5x speedup over ForwardDiff when parameters > chunk size (12)","title":"Tips"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/architecture/#Architecture","page":"Architecture","text":"Newtrinos.jl is organized into three orthogonal layers that enforce separation of concerns.","title":"Architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/architecture/#Three-Layer-Design","page":"Architecture","text":"┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                  Analysis Layer                  │\n│   scan, profile, find_mle, nested sampling      │\n│   Treats experiments as black boxes              │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│               Experiment Layer                   │\n│   deepcore, dayabay, kamland, minos, ...         │\n│   Each defines forward_model, params, priors     │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│                 Physics Layer                    │\n│   osc, atm_flux, earth_layers, xsec             │\n│   Theory predictions, no experiment knowledge    │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘","title":"Three-Layer Design"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/architecture/#Physics-Layer-(src/physics/)","page":"Architecture","text":"Theory predictions with no experiment knowledge. Each module returns a struct <: Newtrinos.Physics with params, priors, and callable functions.\n\nosc.jl — Neutrino oscillation probability engine\nearth_layers.jl — PREM Earth density model\natm_flux.jl — Atmospheric neutrino fluxes (HKKM with Barr systematics)\nxsec.jl — Cross-section models\ncevns_xsec.jl, sns_flux.jl — COHERENT-specific physics","title":"Physics Layer (src/physics/)"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/architecture/#Experiment-Layer-(src/experiments/)","page":"Architecture","text":"Each experiment module has configure(physics=default_physics()) returning a struct <: Newtrinos.Experiment with fields: physics, params, priors, assets, forward_model, plot.","title":"Experiment Layer (src/experiments/)"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/architecture/#Analysis-Layer-(src/analysis/)","page":"Architecture","text":"Inference tools treating experiments as black boxes:\n\nanalysis_tools.jl — NewtrinosResult, find_mle, profile, scan, generate_likelihood\nmolewhacker.jl — Adaptive importance sampling\ncli_common.jl — Shared CLI utilities","title":"Analysis Layer (src/analysis/)"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/architecture/#Parameter-Flow","page":"Architecture","text":"Parameters flow as NamedTuples throughout the codebase. get_params/get_priors merge across all physics and experiment modules using safe_merge (which checks for conflicts). Use @reset from Accessors.jl to modify individual fields.","title":"Parameter Flow"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/architecture/#ForwardDiff-Compatibility","page":"Architecture","text":"The oscillation code runs in the inner loop of gradient-based optimization. All code avoids Float64 literals that would strip Dual numbers; uses zero(T), one(T), promote_type instead.","title":"ForwardDiff Compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Installation","page":"Getting Started","text":"Newtrinos.jl is not yet registered in the Julia General registry. Install it directly from GitHub:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/philippeller/Newtrinos.jl.git\")\n\nOr for development:\n\ngit clone https://github.com/philippeller/Newtrinos.jl.git\ncd Newtrinos.jl\njulia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#First-Steps","page":"Getting Started","text":"Load the package:\n\nusing Newtrinos\nusing DensityInterface\n\nConfigure an experiment with default physics:\n\nexp = Newtrinos.dayabay.configure()\n\nEach experiment returns a struct containing physics models, parameters, priors, data assets, and a forward model. Extract parameters and evaluate the likelihood:\n\nexperiments = (; dayabay = exp)\nparams = Newtrinos.get_params(experiments)\nlikelihood = Newtrinos.generate_likelihood(experiments)\n\nlogdensityof(likelihood, params)","title":"First Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Running-Tests","page":"Getting Started","text":"julia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'","title":"Running Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Next-Steps","page":"Getting Started","text":"Single Experiment — configure and explore one experiment in depth\nJoint Analysis — combine experiments and run scans\nArchitecture — understand the three-layer design","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cli/#CLI-Reference","page":"CLI Reference","text":"The main analysis entry point is src/analysis/analysis.jl.","title":"CLI Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cli/#Usage","page":"CLI Reference","text":"julia --project src/analysis/analysis.jl \\\n    --experiments deepcore dayabay \\\n    --name myrun \\\n    --task profile \\\n    --workers 4 \\\n    --threads 2 \\\n    --plot","title":"Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cli/#Arguments","page":"CLI Reference","text":"Argument Required Default Description\n--experiments Yes — Space-separated list of experiments\n--name Yes — Base name for output files (.jld2, .png)\n--task Yes — One of: Scan, Profile, NestedSampling, ImportanceSampling\n--workers No 1 Number of distributed workers\n--threads No 1 Threads per worker (when --workers > 1)\n--plot No false Generate a PNG plot of results","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cli/#Tasks","page":"CLI Reference","text":"","title":"Tasks"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cli/#Scan","page":"CLI Reference","text":"Evaluates the likelihood on a grid of parameter values. Fast but does not account for nuisance parameter variations.\n\njulia -t 4 --project src/analysis/analysis.jl \\\n    --experiments dayabay --name scan_test --task scan","title":"Scan"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cli/#Profile","page":"CLI Reference","text":"Profile likelihood scan: at each grid point, optimizes over all nuisance parameters using LBFGS. Results are cached to disk for resumability.\n\njulia --project src/analysis/analysis.jl \\\n    --experiments deepcore dayabay --name profile_test --task profile --workers 4","title":"Profile"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cli/#NestedSampling","page":"CLI Reference","text":"Bayesian inference via nested sampling (requires UltraNest).","title":"NestedSampling"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cli/#ImportanceSampling","page":"CLI Reference","text":"Adaptive importance sampling via the mole-whacking algorithm.","title":"ImportanceSampling"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cli/#Parallelism","page":"CLI Reference","text":"--workers 1 (default): Grid points parallelized via Julia threads. Control thread count at launch with julia -t N.\n--workers N: Spawns N distributed processes via addprocs. Grid points distributed via pmap. Use --threads M to set threads per worker.","title":"Parallelism"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cli/#Output","page":"CLI Reference","text":"<name>.jld2 — Results file containing a NewtrinosResult struct\n<name>.png — Contour plot (if --plot is set)","title":"Output"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/cli/#Benchmarks","page":"CLI Reference","text":"julia -t 4 --project benchmark/bench_likelihood.jl --experiments deepcore\njulia --project benchmark/bench_osc.jl","title":"Benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Physics-API","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Physics API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Oscillations","page":"Physics","text":"The oscillation module is accessed via Newtrinos.osc. Use Newtrinos.osc.configure to create an Newtrinos.osc.Osc physics module that can be passed to any experiment.","title":"Oscillations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Configuration","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Data-Types","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Data Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Core-Functions","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Core Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Flavour-Models","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Flavour Models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Parameters-and-Priors","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Parameters and Priors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Interaction-Models","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Interaction Models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Propagation-Models","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Propagation Models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#State-Selection","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"State Selection"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Eigendecomposition","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Eigendecomposition"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Analytic-Eigendecomposition","page":"Physics","text":"Closed-form 3×3 Hermitian eigendecomposition underlying Newtrinos.BargerEigen. These functions are defined in src/physics/eigen_hermitian_3x3.jl and live in the top-level Newtrinos namespace.","title":"Analytic Eigendecomposition"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Internal-Functions","page":"Physics","text":"These functions are not part of the public API but are documented for developers working on the oscillation engine.","title":"Internal Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Earth-Layers","page":"Physics","text":"The Earth density module is accessed via Newtrinos.earth_layers. Use Newtrinos.earth_layers.configure to create an Newtrinos.earth_layers.EarthLayers physics module providing layer structures and path computations for matter-effect oscillations.","title":"Earth Layers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Density-Models","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Density Models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Configuration-and-Functions","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Configuration and Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Atmospheric-Flux","page":"Physics","text":"The atmospheric flux module is accessed via Newtrinos.atm_flux. Use Newtrinos.atm_flux.configure to create an Newtrinos.atm_flux.AtmFlux physics module providing nominal HKKM fluxes and Barr systematic modifications.","title":"Atmospheric Flux"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Flux-Models","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Flux Models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Configuration-and-Functions-2","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Configuration and Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Internal-Functions-2","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Internal Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#SNS-Flux","page":"Physics","text":"The Spallation Neutron Source flux module is accessed via Newtrinos.sns_flux. Use Newtrinos.sns_flux.configure to create an Newtrinos.sns_flux.SNSFlux physics module for COHERENT-style experiments.","title":"SNS Flux"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Configuration-2","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Internal-Functions-3","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Internal Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#CEvNS-Cross-Section","page":"Physics","text":"The CEvNS cross-section module is accessed via Newtrinos.cevns_xsec. Use Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.configure to create a Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.CevnsXsec physics module for COHERENT-style experiments.","title":"CEvNS Cross-Section"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Configuration-3","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Internal-Functions-4","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Internal Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Cross-Sections","page":"Physics","text":"The cross-section module is accessed via Newtrinos.xsec. Use Newtrinos.xsec.configure to create a Newtrinos.xsec.Xsec physics module.","title":"Cross-Sections"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Models","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Configuration-and-Functions-3","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Configuration and Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/physics/#Utility-Functions","page":"Physics","text":"","title":"Utility Functions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.OscillationConfig","page":"Physics","text":"OscillationConfig{F, I, P, S, E}\n\nMaster configuration for the neutrino oscillation probability engine.\n\nEach type parameter selects a different physical model via multiple dispatch. Construct with keyword arguments and pass to configure to obtain a ready-to-use Osc physics module.\n\nFields\n\nflavour::F = ThreeFlavour(): neutrino mass/flavour model (FlavourModel).\ninteraction::I = Vacuum(): matter interaction model (InteractionModel).\npropagation::P = Basic(): propagation mechanism (PropagationModel).\nstates::S = All(): eigenstate selection strategy (StateSelector).\neigen_method::E = DefaultEigen(): eigendecomposition algorithm (EigenMethod).\n\nExamples\n\n# Standard 3-flavour vacuum oscillations (all defaults)\ncfg = OscillationConfig()\n\n# Inverted ordering with MSW matter effects\ncfg = OscillationConfig(flavour=ThreeFlavour(ordering=:IO), interaction=SI())\n\n# ADD model with decoherence and Barger eigensolver\ncfg = OscillationConfig(\n    flavour=ADD(N_KK=3),\n    interaction=SI(),\n    propagation=Decoherent(σₑ=0.05),\n    eigen_method=BargerEigen()\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.OscillationConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.Osc","page":"Physics","text":"Osc <: Newtrinos.Physics\n\nConfigured oscillation physics module, returned by configure.\n\nThis struct satisfies the Newtrinos.Physics interface and can be passed into any experiment's configure(physics=...) method.\n\nFields\n\ncfg::OscillationConfig: the full configuration used to build this module.\nparams::NamedTuple: default oscillation parameter values (e.g. θ₁₂, Δm²₃₁, …).\npriors::NamedTuple: prior distributions for each parameter.\nmatrices::Function: closure matrices(params) -> (U, h) returning mixing matrix and mass-squared eigenvalues.\nosc_prob::Function: closure computing oscillation probabilities. See get_osc_prob for the two call signatures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.Osc"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.configure","page":"Physics","text":"configure(cfg::OscillationConfig=OscillationConfig()) -> Osc\n\nCreate a fully configured oscillation physics module from the given configuration.\n\nAssembles default parameters, priors, mixing-matrix closure, and oscillation-probability closure into an Osc struct ready for use in experiment forward models.\n\nArguments\n\ncfg::OscillationConfig: oscillation configuration (defaults to standard 3-flavour vacuum).\n\nReturns\n\nAn Osc instance.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Newtrinos\n\n# Default 3-flavour vacuum\nphysics = Newtrinos.osc.configure()\n\n# With matter effects and inverted ordering\nphysics = Newtrinos.osc.configure(\n    OscillationConfig(flavour=ThreeFlavour(ordering=:IO), interaction=SI())\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.configure"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.ftype","page":"Physics","text":"ftype\n\nType alias for Float64, used as the default floating-point precision throughout the oscillation module.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.ftype"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.Layer","page":"Physics","text":"Layer{T}\n\nA spherical shell of matter with uniform proton and neutron number densities.\n\nUsed to represent concentric layers of the Earth in the PREM density model (see Newtrinos.earth_layers.EarthLayers in earth_layers.jl). A neutrino trajectory through the Earth is described by a sequence of Path segments, each referencing one layer.\n\nFields\n\nradius::T: outer radius of the shell [km].\np_density::T: proton number density [mol/cm^3].\nn_density::T: neutron number density [mol/cm^3].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.Layer"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.Path","page":"Physics","text":"Path\n\nA single segment of a neutrino propagation path through one matter Layer.\n\nA full baseline is represented as a Vector{Path}, one entry per layer traversed. For vacuum oscillations the total baseline is simply sum(segment.length for segment in path).\n\nFields\n\nlength::Float64: segment length [km].\nlayer_idx::Int: index into the corresponding StructVector{Layer}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.Path"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.get_osc_prob","page":"Physics","text":"get_osc_prob(cfg::OscillationConfig) -> Function\n\nConstruct the oscillation probability closure for the given configuration.\n\nThe returned function osc_prob has two methods:\n\nVacuum-style (baselines as distances):\n\nosc_prob(E::AbstractVector, L::AbstractVector, params::NamedTuple; anti=false)\n\nMatter-style (baselines as Earth layer paths):\n\nosc_prob(E::AbstractVector, paths::VectorOfVectors{Path}, layers::StructVector{Layer},\n         params::NamedTuple; anti=false)\n\nArguments\n\nE: neutrino energies [GeV].\nL: baselines [km] (vacuum method).\npaths: per-baseline layer traversals (matter method), from Newtrinos.earth_layers.EarthLayers.\nlayers: Earth density layers (matter method).\nparams::NamedTuple: oscillation parameters (mixing angles, mass splittings, etc.).\nanti::Bool = false: set true for antineutrinos (conjugates the PMNS matrix).\n\nReturns\n\nArray{T,4} of shape (n_E, n_L, n_flav, n_flav) where entry result[i, j, β, α] gives the transition probability P(nu_alpha to nu_beta) at energy E[i] and baseline/path index j.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.get_osc_prob"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.get_matrices","page":"Physics","text":"get_matrices(cfg::FlavourModel, eigen_method::EigenMethod=DefaultEigen()) -> Function\n\nConstruct a closure that computes the mixing matrix and mass-squared eigenvalues from oscillation parameters.\n\nThe returned function has signature matrices(params::NamedTuple) -> (U, h) where:\n\nU is the mixing matrix (unitary).\nh is a vector of mass-squared eigenvalues [eV²].\n\nFor ThreeFlavour, returns SMatrix{3,3} and SVector{3} (zero-allocation). For Sterile, returns 4×4 dense matrices. For ADD and dark-dimension models, returns 3(N_KK+1) times 3(N_KK+1) dense matrices obtained by diagonalizing the full KK mass matrix via decompose.\n\nThe closure is ForwardDiff-compatible: all intermediate computations preserve dual-number types through zero(T) / one(T) patterns and type promotion.\n\nArguments\n\ncfg::FlavourModel: flavour model instance (dispatches to the appropriate method).\neigen_method::EigenMethod: eigendecomposition algorithm (only used by models that diagonalize a mass matrix, e.g. ADD).\n\nSee also get_PMNS, configure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.get_matrices"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.get_PMNS","page":"Physics","text":"get_PMNS(params) -> SMatrix{3,3}\n\nConstruct the 3 times 3 PMNS leptonic mixing matrix from oscillation parameters.\n\nThe matrix is built as\n\nU_PMNS = R_23(theta_23) cdot R_13(theta_13 delta_CP) cdot R_12(theta_12)\n\nwhere each rotation matrix uses zero(T) / one(T) to preserve ForwardDiff.Dual number types for automatic differentiation.\n\nArguments\n\nparams::NamedTuple: must contain θ₂₃, θ₁₃, θ₁₂, and δCP.\n\nReturns\n\nSMatrix{3,3,Complex{T}} — the PMNS mixing matrix, where T is inferred from the parameter types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.get_PMNS"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.get_abs_masses","page":"Physics","text":"get_abs_masses(params) -> (m1, m2, m3)\n\nCompute the absolute neutrino masses from the lightest mass and mass-squared splittings.\n\nFor normal ordering (Delta m^2_31  0): m_1 = m_0, m_2 = sqrtDelta m^2_21 + m_0^2, m_3 = sqrtDelta m^2_31 + m_0^2.\n\nFor inverted ordering (Delta m^2_31  0): m_3 = m_0, m_1 = sqrt-Delta m^2_31 + m_0^2, m_2 = sqrtDelta m^2_21 - Delta m^2_31 + m_0^2.\n\nArguments\n\nparams::NamedTuple: must contain m₀, Δm²₂₁, and Δm²₃₁.\n\nReturns\n\nA tuple (m1, m2, m3) of absolute neutrino masses [eV].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.get_abs_masses"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.FlavourModel","page":"Physics","text":"FlavourModel\n\nAbstract type selecting the neutrino mass/flavour model.\n\nEach subtype defines its own set of oscillation parameters (mixing angles, mass splittings, and any BSM quantities) via get_params and get_priors dispatches, as well as a get_matrices method that returns the mixing matrix U and mass-squared eigenvalues.\n\nStandard and BSM subtypes:\n\nThreeFlavour: standard three-neutrino oscillations.\nThreeFlavourXYCP: three-flavour with shell parametrization of delta_CP.\nSterile: 3+1 sterile neutrino model.\nADD: Arkani-Hamed–Dimopoulos–Dvali large extra dimensions.\nDarkdim_Lambda, Darkdim_Masses, Darkdim_cas: dark-dimension model variants.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.FlavourModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.ThreeFlavour","page":"Physics","text":"ThreeFlavour <: FlavourModel\n\nStandard three-flavour neutrino oscillation model.\n\nThe PMNS mixing matrix is constructed as U_PMNS = R_23(theta_23) cdot R_13(theta_13 delta_CP) cdot R_12(theta_12) and the oscillation parameters are:\n\nParameter Description Default\nθ₁₂ Solar mixing angle arcsin(sqrt0307)\nθ₁₃ Reactor mixing angle arcsin(sqrt0021)\nθ₂₃ Atmospheric mixing angle arcsin(sqrt057)\nδCP Dirac CP-violation phase 1.0 rad\nΔm²₂₁ Solar mass-squared splitting [eV²] 753 times 10^-5\nΔm²₃₁ Atmospheric mass-squared splitting [eV²] depends on ordering\n\nFields\n\nordering::Symbol = :NO: neutrino mass ordering. :NO for normal ordering (Delta m^2_31  0), :IO for inverted ordering (Delta m^2_31  0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.ThreeFlavour"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.ThreeFlavourXYCP","page":"Physics","text":"ThreeFlavourXYCP <: FlavourModel\n\nThree-flavour model with a shell (Cartesian) parametrization of delta_CP.\n\nReplaces the scalar δCP parameter with a 2-vector δCPshell to avoid discontinuities at the circular boundary 0 2pi) during gradient-based fitting.\n\nFields\n\nthree_flavour::ThreeFlavour = ThreeFlavour(): underlying three-flavour configuration.\n\nSee also ThreeFlavour.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.ThreeFlavourXYCP"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.Sterile","page":"Physics","text":"Sterile <: FlavourModel\n\n3+1 sterile neutrino model extending the standard three flavours.\n\nAdds a fourth mass eigenstate with mass-squared splitting Δm²₄₁ and three new mixing angles θ₁₄, θ₂₄, θ₃₄. The PMNS matrix is extended to 4×4 via\n\nU_4times4 = R_34 R_24 R_14 cdot\nbeginpmatrix U_PMNS  0  0  1 endpmatrix\n\nFields\n\nthree_flavour::ThreeFlavour = ThreeFlavour(): underlying three-flavour configuration.\n\nSee also ThreeFlavour.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.Sterile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.ADD","page":"Physics","text":"ADD <: FlavourModel\n\nArkani-Hamed–Dimopoulos–Dvali (ADD) large extra dimensions model.\n\nIntroduces a tower of N_KK Kaluza-Klein excitations for each active neutrino, yielding a 3(N_KK+1) times 3(N_KK+1) mass matrix. The Dirac mass matrix is constructed from the PMNS matrix and absolute neutrino masses, with off-diagonal couplings controlled by the extra-dimension compactification radius.\n\nAdditional parameters beyond ThreeFlavour:\n\nParameter Description Default\nm₀ Lightest neutrino mass [eV] 0.01\nADD_radius Extra-dimension compactification radius [μm] 0.01\n\nFields\n\nthree_flavour::ThreeFlavour = ThreeFlavour(): underlying three-flavour configuration.\nN_KK::Int = 5: number of Kaluza-Klein modes to include.\n\nSee also Cut for excluding heavy KK states from coherent oscillation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.ADD"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.get_params","page":"Physics","text":"get_params(cfg::OscillationConfig) -> NamedTuple\nget_params(cfg::FlavourModel) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturn the default oscillation parameter values for the given configuration or flavour model.\n\nDelegates to the flavour-model-specific method. Each FlavourModel subtype defines its own set of parameters (mixing angles, mass splittings, and any BSM quantities).\n\nArguments\n\ncfg: an OscillationConfig or a FlavourModel subtype instance.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple of Symbol => Float64 (or Vector for shell parameters) with the nominal parameter values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.get_params"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.get_priors","page":"Physics","text":"get_priors(cfg::OscillationConfig) -> NamedTuple\nget_priors(cfg::FlavourModel) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturn prior distributions for each oscillation parameter.\n\nDelegates to the flavour-model-specific method. Priors are Distributions.jl objects (typically Uniform or LogUniform) used for sampling and inference.\n\nArguments\n\ncfg: an OscillationConfig or a FlavourModel subtype instance.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple of Symbol => Distribution mapping each parameter to its prior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.get_priors"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.InteractionModel","page":"Physics","text":"InteractionModel\n\nAbstract type selecting the neutrino-matter interaction model used when propagating through Earth layers. Subtypes:\n\nVacuum: no matter effects.\nSI: Standard Interactions (MSW effect).\nNSI: Non-Standard Interactions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.InteractionModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.Vacuum","page":"Physics","text":"Vacuum <: InteractionModel\n\nVacuum oscillations — no matter effects. Layer densities in Layer are ignored; the baseline is the sum of Path segment lengths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.Vacuum"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.SI","page":"Physics","text":"SI <: InteractionModel\n\nStandard Interactions — coherent forward scattering in matter (the MSW effect).\n\nAdds the Wolfenstein charged-current potential V_CC = sqrt2 G_F N_e to the (nu_e nu_e) element and a neutral-current potential to all diagonal elements of the effective Hamiltonian. The sign is flipped for antineutrinos.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.SI"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.NSI","page":"Physics","text":"NSI <: InteractionModel\n\nNon-Standard Interactions in matter. Extends the matter Hamiltonian beyond the Standard Model Wolfenstein potential.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.NSI"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.PropagationModel","page":"Physics","text":"PropagationModel\n\nAbstract type governing how neutrino quantum states evolve along the baseline.\n\nSubtypes select different physical approximations for the oscillation amplitude:\n\nBasic: standard coherent quantum-mechanical propagation.\nDecoherent: density-matrix evolution with off-diagonal damping.\nDamping: amplitude-level low-pass filter with incoherent recovery.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.PropagationModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.Basic","page":"Physics","text":"Basic <: PropagationModel\n\nStandard coherent quantum-mechanical propagation (no decoherence effects).\n\nThe oscillation amplitude is computed as\n\nA_alphabeta = bigl(U  mathrmdiagbigl(e^-iDelta m^2_j L  4Ebigr)  U^daggerbigr)_alphabeta\n\nand the transition probability is P_alphabeta = A_alphabeta^2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.Basic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.Decoherent","page":"Physics","text":"Decoherent <: PropagationModel\n\nPropagation with energy-dependent decoherence via density-matrix evolution.\n\nOff-diagonal elements of the density matrix in the mass eigenbasis are damped by\n\nD_ij = expbigl(-2Deltaphi_ijsigma_e^2bigr) qquad\nDeltaphi_ij = fracDelta m^2_ij L4E\n\nafter coherent phase evolution at each layer crossing.\n\nFields\n\nσₑ::Float64 = 0.1: decoherence strength parameter (dimensionless).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.Decoherent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.Damping","page":"Physics","text":"Damping <: PropagationModel\n\nPropagation with a low-pass filter that damps fast-oscillating amplitudes.\n\nEach mass-eigenstate phase factor is multiplied by a decay d_j = exp(-2phi_jsigma_e^2) and the probability is corrected by an incoherent recovery term U^2  mathrmdiag(1-d_j^2)  U^2prime.\n\nFields\n\nσₑ::Float64 = 0.1: damping strength parameter (dimensionless).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.Damping"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.StateSelector","page":"Physics","text":"StateSelector\n\nAbstract type controlling which mass eigenstates contribute to the coherent oscillation calculation. Subtypes:\n\nAll: include every eigenstate (default).\nCut: exclude eigenstates above a mass-squared cutoff.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.StateSelector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.All","page":"Physics","text":"All <: StateSelector\n\nInclude all mass eigenstates in the oscillation calculation. This is the default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.All"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.Cut","page":"Physics","text":"Cut <: StateSelector\n\nExclude mass eigenstates whose sqrtm^2_j exceeds a cutoff threshold.\n\nStates above the cutoff are averaged incoherently, which is useful for ADD or dark-dimension models where heavy Kaluza-Klein states oscillate too rapidly to be resolved experimentally.\n\nFields\n\ncutoff::Float64 = Inf: mass eigenvalue cutoff [eV]. Default Inf keeps all states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.Cut"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.EigenMethod","page":"Physics","text":"EigenMethod\n\nAbstract type selecting the eigendecomposition algorithm for the effective matter Hamiltonian. Subtypes:\n\nDefaultEigen: Julia's built-in LinearAlgebra.eigen.\nBargerEigen (defined in barger_eigen.jl): fast analytic 3×3 decomposition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.EigenMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.DefaultEigen","page":"Physics","text":"DefaultEigen <: EigenMethod\n\nUse Julia's built-in LinearAlgebra.eigen for Hermitian matrix diagonalization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.DefaultEigen"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.decompose","page":"Physics","text":"decompose(H::Hermitian, method::EigenMethod) -> Eigen\n\nEigendecompose the Hermitian Hamiltonian H using the selected EigenMethod.\n\nThis is the extension point for custom eigensolvers. To add a new method, define a subtype of EigenMethod and a corresponding decompose dispatch.\n\nArguments\n\nH::Hermitian: effective Hamiltonian matrix (mass-squared basis or matter-modified).\nmethod::EigenMethod: algorithm selector.\n\nReturns\n\nAn Eigen factorization with fields vectors and values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.decompose"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.BargerEigen","page":"Physics","text":"BargerEigen <: Newtrinos.osc.EigenMethod\n\nAnalytic 3×3 Hermitian eigendecomposition using closed-form expressions. Uses the method of Deledalle et al. 2017, which computes eigenvalues via the cubic formula and eigenvectors via closed-form expressions.\n\nOnly valid for 3×3 Hermitian matrices (i.e. three-flavour oscillations). Falls back to Julia's eigen for non-3×3 matrices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.BargerEigen"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.fast_eigen","page":"Physics","text":"fast_eigen(C::Hermitian{T, <:SMatrix{3,3,T}}; sortby=identity) -> Eigen\n\nAnalytic eigendecomposition of a 3×3 Hermitian matrix using closed-form expressions.\n\nImplements the algorithm of Deledalle et al. (2017), hal-01501221. This avoids the iterative LAPACK eigen call and is significantly faster for the small matrices that arise in three-flavour neutrino oscillations with matter effects.\n\nCalled by Newtrinos.BargerEigen via Newtrinos.osc.decompose.\n\nEigenvalue algorithm: Computes the three roots of the characteristic polynomial via the Cardano trigonometric method. With x_1 and x_2 defined from the matrix elements, the discriminant Delta = 4x_1^3 - x_2^2 geq 0 and the roots are\n\nlambda_k = fraca+b+c3 + frac2sqrtx_13\ncosleft(fracphi + 2pi(k-1)3right) quad k = 123\n\nwhere phi = operatornameatan(sqrtDelta x_2).\n\nEigenvector algorithm: Each eigenvector is obtained as the cross product of two rows of (C - lambda_k I). Three row-pair combinations are tried in order to handle degenerate or near-degenerate cases where a cross product vanishes.\n\nDispatches to specialized helpers _fast_eigen_efzero, _fast_eigen_edzero, _fast_eigen_dfzero when pairs of off-diagonal elements are zero (reducing to 2×2 subproblems).\n\nThe matrix elements are labelled as follows (a, b, c are real diagonal entries; d, e, f are complex lower-triangle entries):\n\nC = beginpmatrix\n      a        d^*      f^* \n      d        b        e^* \n      f        e        c\n    endpmatrix\n\nArguments\n\nC::Hermitian{T, <:SMatrix{3,3,T}}: the 3×3 Hermitian matrix to decompose.\nsortby::F = identity: ordering function applied to eigenvalues (e.g. abs, real).\n\nReturns\n\nAn Eigen object with values::SVector{3} and vectors::SMatrix{3,3}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.fast_eigen"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos._fast_eigen_efzero","page":"Physics","text":"_fast_eigen_efzero(C; sortby=identity) -> Eigen\n\nEdge-case eigendecomposition when C[3,1] == C[3,2] == 0 (off-diagonal elements e=f=0).\n\nRow 3 decouples: lambda_3 = C_33 with eigenvector (0 0 1). The remaining two eigenvalues and vectors are obtained by calling Julia's eigen on the 2×2 upper-left submatrix C_1212, then embedding the result back into 3×3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos._fast_eigen_efzero"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos._fast_eigen_edzero","page":"Physics","text":"_fast_eigen_edzero(C; sortby=identity) -> Eigen\n\nEdge-case eigendecomposition when C[1,2] == C[2,3] == 0 (off-diagonal elements e=d=0).\n\nRow 2 decouples: lambda_2 = C_22 with eigenvector (0 1 0). The remaining two eigenvalues are found from the 2×2 submatrix of rows/columns 1 and 3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos._fast_eigen_edzero"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos._fast_eigen_dfzero","page":"Physics","text":"_fast_eigen_dfzero(C; sortby=identity) -> Eigen\n\nEdge-case eigendecomposition when C[1,2] == C[1,3] == 0 (off-diagonal elements d=f=0).\n\nRow 1 decouples: lambda_1 = C_11 with eigenvector (1 0 0). The remaining two eigenvalues are found from the 2×2 lower-right submatrix C_2323.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos._fast_eigen_dfzero"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos._sorted_eigen","page":"Physics","text":"_sorted_eigen(::Nothing, vals, vecs) -> Eigen\n\nPassthrough overload that wraps eigenvalues and eigenvectors into an Eigen object without reordering. Called when sortby = nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n_sorted_eigen(by::F, vals, vecs) -> Eigen\n\nSort eigenvalues by the key function by and permute eigenvector columns accordingly.\n\nArguments\n\nby::F: ordering key function (e.g. identity, abs, real).\nvals: eigenvalue vector.\nvecs: eigenvector matrix (columns correspond to eigenvalues).\n\nReturns\n\nAn Eigen object with eigenvalues sorted by by and eigenvectors permuted to match.\n\n\n\n\n\n_sorted_eigen(by::Fun, F::Eigen) -> Eigen\n\nConvenience overload that unpacks an Eigen object and delegates to _sorted_eigen(by, F.values, F.vectors).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos._sorted_eigen"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.osc_kernel","page":"Physics","text":"osc_kernel(U, H, e, l) -> AbstractMatrix\n\nCompute the coherent oscillation amplitude matrix for a single energy and baseline.\n\nEvaluates A = U  mathrmdiagbigl(e^-i phi_jbigr)  U^dagger where phi_j = fracDelta m^2_j  L4E (in natural units via F_units).\n\nArguments\n\nU::AbstractMatrix{<:Number}: mixing matrix (unitary, n times n).\nH::AbstractVector{<:Number}: mass-squared eigenvalues [eV²].\ne::Real: neutrino energy [GeV].\nl::Real: baseline length [km].\n\nReturns\n\nAn n times n complex amplitude matrix A_betaalpha.\n\n\n\n\n\nosc_kernel(U, H, e, l, σₑ) -> (A, decay)\n\nCompute the oscillation amplitude matrix with low-pass damping.\n\nSame as the coherent osc_kernel but each phase factor is additionally multiplied by a decay d_j = exp(-2phi_jsigma_e^2).\n\nArguments\n\nU::AbstractMatrix{<:Number}: mixing matrix.\nH::AbstractVector{<:Number}: mass-squared eigenvalues [eV²].\ne::Real: neutrino energy [GeV].\nl::Real: baseline length [km].\nσₑ::Real: damping strength parameter.\n\nReturns\n\nA tuple (A, decay) where A is the damped amplitude matrix and decay is the per-eigenstate decay vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.osc_kernel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.compute_matter_matrices","page":"Physics","text":"compute_matter_matrices(H_eff, e, layer, anti, interaction::SI, eigen_method=DefaultEigen()) -> (U, h)\n\nAdd the MSW matter potential to the effective Hamiltonian and diagonalize.\n\nModifies H_eff by adding the Wolfenstein charged-current and neutral-current potentials for the given Layer density, then eigendecomposes the result via decompose. Two methods are provided: one for generic AbstractMatrix and an optimized one for SMatrix{3,3}.\n\nArguments\n\nH_eff: effective Hamiltonian in the flavour basis (Umathrmdiag(h)U^dagger).\ne::Real: neutrino energy [GeV].\nlayer::Layer: matter layer with proton and neutron densities.\nanti::Bool: true for antineutrinos (flips potential sign).\ninteraction::SI: matter interaction selector.\neigen_method::EigenMethod: eigendecomposition algorithm.\n\nReturns\n\nA tuple (U_m, h_m) of matter-modified eigenvectors and eigenvalues.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.compute_matter_matrices"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.osc_reduce","page":"Physics","text":"osc_reduce(matter_matrices, path, e, propagation) -> Matrix\n\nCombine per-layer oscillation amplitudes along a multi-layer path into a single transition probability matrix for one energy.\n\nDispatches on PropagationModel:\n\nBasic: multiplies amplitude matrices across layers, then takes cdot^2.\nDamping: multiplies damped amplitudes and adds an incoherent recovery term using a path-length-weighted average of U^2.\n\nArguments\n\nmatter_matrices: vector of (U_m, h_m) tuples, one per layer (from compute_matter_matrices).\npath::Vector{Path}: sequence of path segments for this baseline.\ne::Real: neutrino energy [GeV].\npropagation::PropagationModel: propagation model instance.\n\nReturns\n\nAn n times n real matrix of transition probabilities P_betaalpha.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.osc_reduce"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.matter_osc_per_e","page":"Physics","text":"matter_osc_per_e(H_eff, e, layers, paths, anti, propagation, interaction, eigen_method=DefaultEigen()) -> Array\n\nCompute matter-affected oscillation probabilities at a single energy across all paths.\n\nFor each Layer, computes the matter-modified eigensystem via compute_matter_matrices, then reduces along each path via osc_reduce (for Basic/Damping) or via explicit density-matrix evolution (for Decoherent).\n\nArguments\n\nH_eff: effective Hamiltonian in the flavour basis.\ne::Real: neutrino energy [GeV].\nlayers::StructVector{Layer}: Earth density layers.\npaths: per-baseline layer traversals (VectorOfVectors{Path}).\nanti::Bool: true for antineutrinos.\npropagation::PropagationModel: propagation model.\ninteraction::InteractionModel: matter interaction model.\neigen_method::EigenMethod: eigendecomposition algorithm.\n\nReturns\n\nAn Array of shape (n_flav, n_flav, n_paths) with P_betaalpha for each path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.matter_osc_per_e"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.select","page":"Physics","text":"select(U, h, cfg::StateSelector) -> (U_sel, h_sel, rest)\n\nPartition mass eigenstates into coherent and incoherent subsets based on the StateSelector strategy.\n\nAll: returns all states unchanged with rest = 0.0.\nCut: states with sqrtm^2_j below the cutoff are kept for coherent oscillation; states above it are averaged incoherently and their contribution is returned as the rest matrix.\n\nArguments\n\nU: mixing matrix (n_flav times n_states).\nh: mass-squared eigenvalues vector.\ncfg::StateSelector: selection strategy.\n\nReturns\n\nA tuple (U_sel, h_sel, rest) where:\n\nU_sel: mixing matrix columns for coherent states.\nh_sel: eigenvalues for coherent states.\nrest: incoherent probability correction matrix (scalar 0.0 or n times n matrix).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.select"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.osc.propagate","page":"Physics","text":"propagate(U, h, E, L, propagation) -> Array{T,4}\npropagate(U, h, E, paths, layers, propagation, interaction, anti, ...) -> Array{T,4}\n\nCompute oscillation probabilities over grids of energy and baseline/path.\n\nVacuum methods (dispatched by baseline vector L): Loops over all (E, L) pairs, evaluating osc_kernel and squaring amplitudes. Dispatches on PropagationModel for Basic, Damping, and Decoherent propagation.\n\nMatter methods (dispatched by paths::VectorOfVectors{Path}):\n\nVacuum interaction: collapses paths to total lengths and delegates to the vacuum method.\nSI/NSI: reconstructs the flavour-basis Hamiltonian and calls matter_osc_per_e for each energy.\n\nArguments\n\nU: mixing matrix.\nh: mass-squared eigenvalues [eV²].\nE: neutrino energies [GeV].\nL: baselines [km] (vacuum) or paths/layers (matter).\npropagation::PropagationModel: propagation model.\ninteraction::InteractionModel: matter interaction model (matter methods only).\nanti::Bool: true for antineutrinos (matter methods only).\n\nReturns\n\nArray{T,4} of shape (n_flav, n_flav, n_E, n_L) with P_betaalpha (note: the caller get_osc_prob transposes this to (n_E, n_L, n_flav, n_flav)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.osc.propagate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.earth_layers.DensityModel","page":"Physics","text":"DensityModel\n\nAbstract type for Earth density profile models.\n\nEach subtype provides a recipe for dividing the Earth into concentric shells of constant density. Currently the only implementation is PREM.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.earth_layers.DensityModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.earth_layers.PREM","page":"Physics","text":"PREM <: DensityModel\n\nPreliminary Reference Earth Model (Dziewonski & Anderson, 1981).\n\nReads the tabulated PREM density profile from PREM_1s.csv and groups radial shells into zones defined by density boundaries. The proton-to-nucleon fraction is assumed constant across all layers.\n\nFields\n\nzones::Array{Float64} = [0., 4., 7.5, 12.5, 13.1]: density boundaries [g/cm³] defining the constant-density zones. Adjacent PREM rows whose density falls within the same bin are averaged into a single Newtrinos.osc.Layer.\np_fractions::Float64 = 0.5: proton number fraction Y_p = N_p  (N_p + N_n).\natm_heihgt::Float64 = 20.: atmospheric shell thickness [km] added above the Earth's surface (density = 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.earth_layers.PREM"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.earth_layers.EarthLayers","page":"Physics","text":"EarthLayers <: Newtrinos.Physics\n\nConfigured Earth density model, returned by configure.\n\nProvides the layer structure and path-computation functions needed by the oscillation module's matter-effect calculations (see Newtrinos.osc.SI).\n\nFields\n\ncfg::DensityModel: the density model used to build this module.\nparams::NamedTuple: oscillation parameters.\npriors::NamedTuple: prior distributions.\ncompute_layers::Function: closure compute_layers() -> StructVector{Layer} returning the concentric density shells.\ncompute_paths::Function: function compute_paths(cz, layers; r_detector) -> VectorOfVectors{Path} computing the layer traversal for each cosine-zenith value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.earth_layers.EarthLayers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.earth_layers.configure","page":"Physics","text":"configure(cfg::DensityModel=PREM()) -> EarthLayers\n\nCreate a fully configured Earth density physics module.\n\nArguments\n\ncfg::DensityModel: density model (defaults to PREM).\n\nReturns\n\nAn EarthLayers instance with compute_layers and compute_paths closures and empty parameters and priors.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Newtrinos\n\nearth = Newtrinos.earth_layers.configure()\nlayers = earth.compute_layers()\npaths = earth.compute_paths([-1.0, -0.5, 0.0], layers)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.earth_layers.configure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.earth_layers.get_compute_layers","page":"Physics","text":"get_compute_layers(cfg::PREM) -> Function\n\nConstruct a closure that builds the Earth layer structure from the PREM density profile.\n\nThe returned function compute_layers() reads PREM_1s.csv, bins rows by the density boundaries in cfg.zones, and returns a StructVector{Layer} with one entry per zone plus an atmospheric shell.\n\nArguments\n\ncfg::PREM: PREM configuration with zone boundaries, proton fraction, and atmosphere height.\n\nReturns\n\nA zero-argument closure compute_layers() -> StructVector{Layer}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.earth_layers.get_compute_layers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.earth_layers.compute_paths","page":"Physics","text":"compute_paths(cz::Number, layers, r_detector) -> StructArray{Path}\ncompute_paths(cz::AbstractArray, layers; r_detector=6369) -> VectorOfVectors{Path}\n\nCompute the sequence of Newtrinos.osc.Path segments a neutrino traverses through the Earth.\n\nFor a single cosine-zenith value, determines which Newtrinos.osc.Layer shells are crossed using ray_circle_path_length, then builds an ordered list of segments. Layers below the detector are traversed twice (entry and exit), while layers above the detector are traversed once.\n\nThe array method broadcasts over multiple cosine-zenith values and returns a VectorOfVectors{Path}.\n\nArguments\n\ncz: cosine of the zenith angle (scalar or array). −1 is vertically upgoing.\nlayers::StructVector{Layer}: Earth density layers from compute_layers().\nr_detector::Real: radial position of the detector [km] (default 6369, approximate IceCube depth).\n\nReturns\n\nScalar method: StructArray{Path} with length and layer_idx columns.\nArray method: VectorOfVectors{Path}, one Vector{Path} per zenith angle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.earth_layers.compute_paths"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.earth_layers.ray_circle_path_length","page":"Physics","text":"ray_circle_path_length(r, y, cz) -> Real\n\nCompute the chord length of a ray through a circle of radius r.\n\nThe ray originates at radial distance y from the Earth's centre with direction cosine cz (cosine of the zenith angle). Returns zero if the ray does not intersect the circle or if the chord is shorter than 1 km (numerical noise filter).\n\nArguments\n\nr: radius of the spherical shell [km].\ny: radial position of the detector [km].\ncz: cosine of the zenith angle (−1 = vertically upgoing through the core).\n\nReturns\n\nPath length through the shell [km], or zero if no intersection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.earth_layers.ray_circle_path_length"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.NominalFluxModel","page":"Physics","text":"NominalFluxModel\n\nAbstract type for atmospheric neutrino flux predictions.\n\nEach subtype provides a recipe for computing the unoscillated neutrino flux as a function of energy and zenith angle. Currently the only implementation is HKKM.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.NominalFluxModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.HKKM","page":"Physics","text":"HKKM <: NominalFluxModel\n\nHonda–Kajita–Kasahara–Midorikawa (HKKM) atmospheric neutrino flux model.\n\nReads a site-specific flux table (.d file) and constructs cubic-spline interpolations over log_10(E) and costheta_z for each neutrino flavour (nu_mu, barnu_mu, nu_e, barnu_e).\n\nFields\n\nfname::String = \"spl-nu-20-01-000.d\": filename of the HKKM flux table (relative to the physics/ data directory).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.HKKM"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.FluxSystematicsModel","page":"Physics","text":"FluxSystematicsModel\n\nAbstract type for atmospheric flux systematic uncertainty models.\n\nEach subtype defines a set of nuisance parameters and a function that modifies the nominal flux to account for systematic uncertainties. Currently the only implementation is Barr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.FluxSystematicsModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.Barr","page":"Physics","text":"Barr <: FluxSystematicsModel\n\nBarr systematic uncertainty model for atmospheric neutrino fluxes.\n\nParametrizes flux uncertainties as energy- and zenith-dependent modifications following Barr et al., \"Uncertainties in Atmospheric Neutrino Fluxes.\" The systematics cover:\n\nParameter Description\natm_flux_nuenuebar_sigma nu_e  barnu_e ratio uncertainty\natm_flux_numunumubar_sigma nu_mu  barnu_mu ratio uncertainty\natm_flux_nuenumu_sigma nu_e  nu_mu ratio uncertainty\natm_flux_delta_spectral_index Spectral index tilt\natm_flux_uphorizonzal_sigma Up/horizontal anisotropy\natm_flux_updown_sigma Up/down asymmetry\n\nAll sigma parameters are centred at 0 with unit Gaussian priors (truncated at ±3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.Barr"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.AtmFluxConfig","page":"Physics","text":"AtmFluxConfig{F, S}\n\nConfiguration for the atmospheric neutrino flux module.\n\nFields\n\nnominal_model::F = HKKM(): nominal flux prediction model (NominalFluxModel).\nsystematics_model::S = Barr(): systematic uncertainty model (FluxSystematicsModel).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.AtmFluxConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.AtmFlux","page":"Physics","text":"AtmFlux <: Newtrinos.Physics\n\nConfigured atmospheric flux physics module, returned by configure.\n\nFields\n\ncfg::AtmFluxConfig: the configuration used to build this module.\nparams::NamedTuple: default systematic parameter values.\npriors::NamedTuple: prior distributions for each systematic parameter.\nnominal_flux::Function: closure nominal_flux(energy, coszen) -> Table returning the unoscillated flux on a fine (E costheta_z) grid.\nsys_flux::Function: closure sys_flux(flux, params) -> NamedTuple applying systematic modifications to the nominal flux and returning per-flavour flux arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.AtmFlux"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.configure","page":"Physics","text":"configure(cfg::AtmFluxConfig=AtmFluxConfig()) -> AtmFlux\n\nCreate a fully configured atmospheric flux physics module.\n\nArguments\n\ncfg::AtmFluxConfig: flux configuration (defaults to HKKM nominal model with Barr systematics).\n\nReturns\n\nAn AtmFlux instance.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Newtrinos\n\n# Default South Pole flux\nflux_physics = Newtrinos.atm_flux.configure()\n\n# Custom site flux file\nflux_physics = Newtrinos.atm_flux.configure(\n    AtmFluxConfig(nominal_model=HKKM(fname=\"spl-nu-20-12-000.d\"))\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.configure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.get_params","page":"Physics","text":"get_params(cfg::FluxSystematicsModel) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturn the default systematic parameter values for the given flux systematics model.\n\nArguments\n\ncfg::FluxSystematicsModel: a Barr instance.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple mapping parameter names to their nominal values (all default to 0.0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.get_params"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.get_priors","page":"Physics","text":"get_priors(cfg::FluxSystematicsModel) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturn prior distributions for each flux systematic parameter.\n\nFor Barr, all sigma parameters use Truncated(Normal(0, 1), -3, 3) and the spectral index uses Truncated(Normal(0, 0.1), -0.3, 0.3).\n\nArguments\n\ncfg::FluxSystematicsModel: a Barr instance.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple of Symbol => Distribution priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.get_priors"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.get_nominal_flux","page":"Physics","text":"get_nominal_flux(cfg::NominalFluxModel) -> Function\n\nConstruct the nominal flux closure for the given flux model.\n\nFor HKKM, returns a function nominal_flux(energy, coszen) -> Table that evaluates the HKKM spline interpolations on a meshgrid of energy [GeV] and coszen values. The returned Table contains columns true_energy, log10_true_energy, true_coszen, and per-flavour flux values (:numu, :numubar, :nue, :nuebar).\n\nArguments\n\ncfg::NominalFluxModel: a HKKM instance.\n\nReturns\n\nA closure nominal_flux(energy, coszen) -> Table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.get_nominal_flux"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.get_sys_flux","page":"Physics","text":"get_sys_flux(cfg::FluxSystematicsModel) -> Function\n\nConstruct the systematic flux modification closure for the given systematics model.\n\nFor Barr, returns a function sys_flux(flux, params) -> NamedTuple that applies the following energy- and zenith-dependent corrections to the nominal flux:\n\nSpectral tilt: (E  E_textpivot)^Deltagamma with E_textpivot approx 241 GeV.\nnu_e  barnu_e ratio scaling via scale_flux.\nnu_mu  barnu_mu ratio scaling via scale_flux.\nnu_e  nu_mu ratio scaling via scale_flux.\nUp/down asymmetry via updown.\nUp/horizontal anisotropy via uphorizontal (separate polynomial uncertainty fits for nu_e and nu_mu).\n\nArguments\n\ncfg::FluxSystematicsModel: a Barr instance.\n\nReturns\n\nA closure sys_flux(flux, params) -> NamedTuple{(:nue, :numu, :nuebar, :numubar)} of modified flux arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.get_sys_flux"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.get_hkkm_flux","page":"Physics","text":"get_hkkm_flux(filename) -> OrderedDict{Symbol, Extrapolation}\n\nRead an HKKM flux table and return cubic-spline interpolations for each flavour.\n\nParses the fixed-format .d file into 20 cosine-zenith chunks of 101 energy bins each, then builds 2D cubic-spline interpolations over (log_10(EtextGeV) costheta_z).\n\nArguments\n\nfilename::String: absolute path to the HKKM flux data file.\n\nReturns\n\nAn OrderedDict with keys :numu, :numubar, :nue, :nuebar, each mapping to a Interpolations.Extrapolation with linear extrapolation boundary conditions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.get_hkkm_flux"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.scale_flux","page":"Physics","text":"scale_flux(A, B, scale) -> (mod_A, mod_B)\n\nScale the ratio between two flux components while conserving their sum.\n\nGiven fluxes A and B, modifies their ratio by the factor scale while keeping A + B constant: A  B = (A  B) cdot textscale.\n\nArguments\n\nA: flux array for the first component.\nB: flux array for the second component.\nscale: multiplicative factor applied to the AB ratio.\n\nReturns\n\nA tuple (mod_A, mod_B) of rescaled flux arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.scale_flux"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.uphorizontal","page":"Physics","text":"uphorizontal(coszen, rel_error) -> Real\n\nCompute the up/horizontal flux anisotropy correction factor.\n\nModels the zenith-dependent flux distortion as the ratio of an ellipse to a circle, where the ellipse semi-axes are a = 1/rel_error and b = rel_error.\n\nArguments\n\ncoszen: cosine of the zenith angle.\nrel_error: relative error parameter controlling the ellipticity.\n\nReturns\n\nA multiplicative correction factor for the flux.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.uphorizontal"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.atm_flux.updown","page":"Physics","text":"updown(coszen, up_down_ratio) -> AbstractArray\n\nCompute the up/down flux asymmetry correction factor.\n\nUses a smooth tanh(3costheta_z) transition to interpolate between 1/up_down_ratio (downgoing) and up_down_ratio (upgoing).\n\nArguments\n\ncoszen: cosine of the zenith angle (scalar or array).\nup_down_ratio: ratio of upgoing to downgoing flux modification.\n\nReturns\n\nA multiplicative correction factor array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.atm_flux.updown"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.sns_flux.SNSFlux","page":"Physics","text":"SNSFlux <: Newtrinos.Physics\n\nConfigured Spallation Neutron Source (SNS) neutrino flux module.\n\nSupports two modes: data-driven (use_data=true), which loads 2D energy-time flux tables from CSV files, and analytic (use_data=false), which computes fluxes from pion-at-rest decay kinematics.\n\nFields\n\nparams::NamedTuple: flux normalization parameters (flux_norm or sns_nu_per_POT (sns neutrinos per Proton On Target)).\npriors::NamedTuple: prior distributions for each parameter.\nassets::NamedTuple: precomputed energy grids, time bins, and raw flux arrays.\nflux::Function: closure flux(params) -> NamedTuple returning the scaled flux components and total flux.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.sns_flux.SNSFlux"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.sns_flux.configure","page":"Physics","text":"configure(; exposure, distance, ecut, E_bin_width, tcut, use_data, flux_folder, beam_power, proton_energy) -> SNSFlux\n\nCreate a fully configured SNS flux physics module.\n\nArguments\n\nexposure: integrated exposure [GWh].\ndistance: source-to-detector distance [m].\necut::Real = mmu/2: maximum neutrino energy [MeV] (default: m_mu  2).\nE_bin_width::Real = 0.5: energy bin width [MeV] (analytic mode only).\ntcut::Real = 6000.0: maximum time [ns] (data mode only).\nuse_data::Bool = true: if true, load flux from CSV tables (default); if false, use analytic decay-at-rest spectra.\nflux_folder::String: path to the directory containing the SNS flux CSV files.\nbeam_power::Real = 1.4: beam power [MW] (for time normalization).\nproton_energy::Real = 1.0: proton beam energy [GeV] (analytic mode only).\n\nReturns\n\nAn SNSFlux instance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.sns_flux.configure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.sns_flux.get_params","page":"Physics","text":"get_params(use_data::Bool) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturn default flux normalization parameters.\n\nData mode (use_data=true): returns (flux_norm=1.0,).\nAnalytic mode (use_data=false): returns (sns_nu_per_POT=0.09,).\n\nArguments\n\nuse_data::Bool: flux mode (data/analytic) selector.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple with a single normalization parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.sns_flux.get_params"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.sns_flux.get_priors","page":"Physics","text":"get_priors(use_data::Bool) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturn prior distributions for the flux normalization parameter.\n\nData mode: Truncated(Normal(1.0, 0.1), 0.7, 1.3) for flux_norm.\nAnalytic mode: Truncated(Normal(0.09, 0.009), 0.05, 0.15) for sns_nu_per_POT.\n\nArguments\n\nuse_data::Bool: flux mode (data/analytic) selector.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple of Symbol => Distribution priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.sns_flux.get_priors"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.sns_flux.get_assets","page":"Physics","text":"get_assets(; use_data, exposure, distance, beam_power, proton_energy, flux_folder, ecut, E_bin_width, tcut) -> NamedTuple\n\nPrecompute energy grids, time bins, and raw (unnormalized) flux arrays.\n\nIn data mode, reads 2D (E t) flux CSV files, applies energy and time cuts, rebins time into logarithmically spaced bins, and scales by the geometric factor eta = textexposure cdot s_textper GWh  (4pi d^2).\n\nIn analytic mode, evaluates decay-at-rest spectra via flux_nu_mu, flux_nu_e, and flux_nu_mu_bar.\n\nArguments\n\nuse_data::Bool: flux mode selector.\nexposure: integrated exposure [GWh].\ndistance: source-to-detector distance [m].\nbeam_power: beam power [MW].\nproton_energy: proton beam energy [GeV].\nflux_folder: path to CSV flux directory.\necut: energy cutoff [MeV].\nE_bin_width: energy bin width [MeV] (analytic mode).\ntcut: time cutoff [ns] (data mode).\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple with fields E, T (data mode only), and per-flavour flux arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.sns_flux.get_assets"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.sns_flux.get_flux","page":"Physics","text":"get_flux(use_data, assets) -> Function\n\nConstruct the flux evaluation closure from precomputed assets.\n\nThe returned closure flux(params) -> NamedTuple applies the normalization parameter to the raw flux arrays and returns per-flavour fluxes and the total flux.\n\nIn data mode, the returned NamedTuple has fields: E, T, total_flux, flux_e, flux_mu, flux_mu_bar, flux_e_bar.\n\nIn analytic mode: E, total_flux, flux_e, flux_mu, flux_mu_bar.\n\nArguments\n\nuse_data::Bool: flux mode (data/analytic) selector.\nassets::NamedTuple: precomputed energy grids and flux arrays from get_assets.\n\nReturns\n\nA closure flux(params) -> NamedTuple.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.sns_flux.get_flux"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.sns_flux.flux_nu_mu","page":"Physics","text":"flux_nu_mu(E, E0, eta, bin_width) -> (E, flux)\n\nCompute the nu_mu flux from pi^+ to mu^+ nu_mu decay at rest.\n\nThe nu_mu is monoenergetic at E_0 = (m_pi^2 - m_mu^2)(2 m_pi), represented as a Gaussian-smeared delta function binned onto the energy grid.\n\nArguments\n\nE: energy grid [MeV].\nE0: pion decay-at-rest nu_mu energy [MeV].\neta: geometric normalization factor.\nbin_width: energy bin width [MeV].\n\nReturns\n\nA tuple (E, flux) where flux is the bin-integrated nu_mu flux array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.sns_flux.flux_nu_mu"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.sns_flux.flux_nu_e","page":"Physics","text":"flux_nu_e(E, eta, Emax, bin_width) -> (E, flux)\n\nCompute the nu_e flux from mu^+ to e^+ nu_e barnu_mu decay at rest.\n\nThe spectrum follows the Michel distribution: fracdPhidE propto 192 fracE^2m_mu^3 left(frac12 - fracEm_muright) for 0 le E le E_textmax.\n\nArguments\n\nE: energy grid [MeV].\neta: geometric normalization factor.\nEmax: maximum neutrino energy [MeV] (approx m_mu  2).\nbin_width: energy bin width [MeV].\n\nReturns\n\nA tuple (E, flux) where flux is the bin-integrated nu_e flux array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.sns_flux.flux_nu_e"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.sns_flux.flux_nu_mu_bar","page":"Physics","text":"flux_nu_mu_bar(E, eta, Emax, bin_width) -> (E, flux)\n\nCompute the barnu_mu flux from mu^+ to e^+ nu_e barnu_mu decay at rest.\n\nThe spectrum follows: fracdPhidE propto 64 fracE^2m_mu^3 left(frac34 - fracEm_muright) for 0 le E le E_textmax.\n\nArguments\n\nE: energy grid [MeV].\neta: geometric normalization factor.\nEmax: maximum neutrino energy [MeV] (approx m_mu  2).\nbin_width: energy bin width [MeV].\n\nReturns\n\nA tuple (E, flux) where flux is the bin-integrated barnu_mu flux array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.sns_flux.flux_nu_mu_bar"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.CevnsXsec","page":"Physics","text":"CevnsXsec <: Newtrinos.Physics\n\nConfigured Coherent Elastic neutrino-Nucleus Scattering (CEvNS) cross-section module.\n\nComputes the differential cross section dsigmadE_r for neutrino scattering off atomic nuclei, including optional beyond-Standard-Model deformations parameterized by cevns_xsec_a through cevns_xsec_d.\n\nFields\n\nparams::NamedTuple: nominal values for sin^2theta_W, the nuclear radii Rn_* (one per isotope), and the four BSM deformation parameters.\npriors::NamedTuple: prior distributions for each parameter.\ndiff_xsec::Function: closure returning the differential cross section. See get_diff_xsec for the call signature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.CevnsXsec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.configure","page":"Physics","text":"configure(isotopes, er_centers, enu_centers) -> CevnsXsec\n\nCreate a CevnsXsec module from a list of isotope descriptors and energy grids.\n\nBuilds cross-section assets and assembles per-isotope parameters and priors via build_params_and_priors and get_assets.\n\nArguments\n\nisotopes: collection of isotope descriptors. Each entry must have the fields:\nRn_key::Symbol: parameter name for this isotope's nuclear radius (e.g. :Rn_Cs133).\nRn_nom::Float64: nominal nuclear radius R_n [fm].\nmass::Float64: nuclear mass [MeV/c²].\nZ::Int: proton number.\nN::Int: neutron number.\nfraction::Float64: isotopic abundance fraction.\ner_centers: nuclear recoil energy grid [MeV].\nenu_centers: neutrino energy grid [MeV].\n\nReturns\n\nA CevnsXsec instance.\n\n\n\n\n\nconfigure(params::NamedTuple, priors::NamedTuple) -> CevnsXsec\n\nCreate a CevnsXsec from externally supplied parameter and prior NamedTuples.\n\nUse this constructor when the parameter set has already been assembled (e.g. by a previous call to build_params_and_priors) and no isotope list is available.\n\nArguments\n\nparams::NamedTuple: parameter values (must include sin2thetaW, cevns_xsec_a–d, and one Rn_* entry per isotope).\npriors::NamedTuple: corresponding prior distributions.\n\nReturns\n\nA CevnsXsec instance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.configure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.build_params_and_priors","page":"Physics","text":"build_params_and_priors(isotopes) -> (NamedTuple, NamedTuple)\n\nAssemble default parameter values and prior distributions for a given set of isotopes.\n\nIncludes the weak mixing angle sin^2theta_W and four BSM deformation parameters cevns_xsec_a through cevns_xsec_d that modify the SM differential cross section.\n\nPer-isotope nuclear radius parameters Rn_* are appended automatically using the Rn_key field of each isotope descriptor.\n\nArguments\n\nisotopes: collection of isotope descriptors; each must have Rn_key::Symbol and Rn_nom::Float64.\n\nReturns\n\nA 2-tuple (params::NamedTuple, priors::NamedTuple).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.build_params_and_priors"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.get_assets","page":"Physics","text":"get_assets(isotopes, er_centers, enu_centers) -> NamedTuple\n\nPackage isotope data and energy grids into an assets NamedTuple for use by get_diff_xsec.\n\nArguments\n\nisotopes: collection of isotope descriptors (same format as configure).\ner_centers: nuclear recoil energy grid [MeV].\nenu_centers: neutrino energy grid [MeV].\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple with fields:\n\nisotopes: Dict{Symbol, NamedTuple} mapping each Rn_key to (mass, Z, N, fraction, Rn_nom).\ner_centers: the recoil energy grid.\nenu_centers: the neutrino energy grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.get_assets"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.ffsq","page":"Physics","text":"ffsq(er, mn, rn) -> Real\n\nSquared Helm nuclear form factor F^2(q^2), evaluated at recoil energy er.\n\nThe momentum transfer is q = sqrt2 M_N E_r and the form factor is\n\nF(q) = frac3j_1(q R_0)q R_0e^-q^2 s^22\n\nwhere j_1 is the spherical Bessel function of the first kind, R_0 = R_n  (hbar c) with hbar c = 1973textMeVfm, and s = 09textfm is the nuclear skin thickness.\n\nArguments\n\ner: nuclear recoil energy [MeV].\nmn: nuclear mass M_N [MeV/c²].\nrn: nuclear radius R_n [fm].\n\nReturns\n\nThe dimensionless form factor squared F^2(q^2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.ffsq"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.ds","page":"Physics","text":"ds(er, enu, params, nupar, Rn_key) -> Matrix\n\nVectorized differential CEvNS cross section dsigmadE_r on an (N_E_r times N_E_nu) grid.\n\nImplements the BSM-extended SM formula:\n\nfracdsigmadE_r = fracG_F^24piM_NF^2(q^2)maxBigl(0\n    Q_W^2bigl(1+a) + (b-1)tfracE_rE_nu + (c-1)tfracM_N E_r2 E_nu^2\n    + (1+d)bigl(tfracE_rE_nubigr)^2bigrBigr)\n\nwhere Q_W = N - (1 - 4sin^2theta_W)Z. At the SM limit (a = b = c = d = 0), the expression reduces to the standard CEvNS formula of Freedman (1974).\n\nArguments\n\ner: recoil energy grid [MeV], shape (n_er,).\nenu: neutrino energy grid [MeV], shape (n_enu,).\nparams::NamedTuple: must include sin2thetaW, cevns_xsec_a–d, and params[Rn_key].\nnupar: 3-tuple (mass, Z, N) — nuclear mass [MeV/c²], proton number, neutron number.\nRn_key::Symbol: key used to look up the nuclear radius R_n in params.\n\nReturns\n\nMatrix of shape (n_er, n_enu) with units [MeV⁻¹].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.ds"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.get_diff_xsec","page":"Physics","text":"get_diff_xsec(assets) -> Function\n\nBuild the differential cross-section closure over all isotopes from pre-computed assets.\n\nThe energy grids and isotope data are captured from assets at construction time. The returned function has signature:\n\ndiff_xsec(params::NamedTuple) -> Dict{Symbol, Matrix}\n\nIt iterates over all isotopes, calling ds for each, and returns a Dict mapping each Rn_key to its (n_E_r times n_E_nu) cross-section matrix.\n\nArguments\n\nassets: NamedTuple produced by get_assets.\n\nReturns\n\nA callable closure params -> Dict{Symbol, Matrix}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.get_diff_xsec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.get_diff_xsec_lar","page":"Physics","text":"get_diff_xsec_lar() -> Function\n\nReturn a closure that calls ds for the liquid-argon (LAr) target.\n\nThe returned function has signature:\n\ndiff_xsec_lar(er_centers, enu_centers, params, nupar, Rn_key) -> Matrix\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.get_diff_xsec_lar"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.get_diff_xsec_csi","page":"Physics","text":"get_diff_xsec_csi() -> Function\n\nReturn a closure that calls ds for the CsI target.\n\nThe returned function has signature:\n\ndiff_xsec_csi(er_centers, enu_centers, params, nupar, Rn_key) -> Matrix\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.cevns_xsec.get_diff_xsec_csi"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.xsec.XsecModel","page":"Physics","text":"XsecModel\n\nAbstract type for neutrino cross-section models.\n\nEach subtype defines how cross-section normalization factors are applied to event rates. Subtypes:\n\nSimpleScaling: global normalization for NC and nu_tau CC channels.\nDifferential_H2O: per-interaction-mode differential scaling for water Cherenkov detectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.xsec.XsecModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.xsec.SimpleScaling","page":"Physics","text":"SimpleScaling <: XsecModel\n\nSimple cross-section model applying global normalization factors.\n\nProvides two nuisance parameters:\n\nnc_norm: neutral-current cross-section scale factor.\nnutau_cc_norm: nu_tau charged-current cross-section scale factor.\n\nAll other flavour/interaction combinations return unit scaling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.xsec.SimpleScaling"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.xsec.Differential_H2O","page":"Physics","text":"Differential_H2O <: XsecModel\n\nDifferential cross-section model for water (H₂O) targets.\n\nProvides per-interaction-mode normalization parameters (cc1p1h_norm, cc2p2h_norm, cc1pi_norm, ccother_norm, ccdis_norm) in addition to nc_norm and nutau_cc_norm. Energy-dependent CC interaction fractions are interpolated from digitized data (T. Wester, Super-K PhD thesis, Figure 4.7) for nu_e and barnu_e separately.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.xsec.Differential_H2O"},{"category":"type","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.xsec.Xsec","page":"Physics","text":"Xsec <: Newtrinos.Physics\n\nConfigured cross-section physics module, returned by configure.\n\nFields\n\ncfg::XsecModel: the cross-section model used to build this module.\nparams::NamedTuple: default normalization parameter values.\npriors::NamedTuple: prior distributions for each parameter.\nscale::Function: closure that computes the cross-section scale factor. See get_scale for the call signatures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.xsec.Xsec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.xsec.configure","page":"Physics","text":"configure(cfg::XsecModel=SimpleScaling()) -> Xsec\n\nCreate a fully configured cross-section physics module.\n\nAssembles default parameters, priors, and the scaling closure into an Xsec struct ready for use in experiment forward models.\n\nArguments\n\ncfg::XsecModel: cross-section model (defaults to SimpleScaling).\n\nReturns\n\nAn Xsec instance.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Newtrinos\n\n# Default simple scaling\nxsec_physics = Newtrinos.xsec.configure()\n\n# Differential water cross-sections for Super-K\nxsec_physics = Newtrinos.xsec.configure(Differential_H2O())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.xsec.configure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.xsec.get_params","page":"Physics","text":"get_params(cfg::XsecModel) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturn the default cross-section normalization parameter values for the given model.\n\nArguments\n\ncfg::XsecModel: a SimpleScaling or Differential_H2O instance.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple mapping parameter names to their nominal values (all default to 1.0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.xsec.get_params"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.xsec.get_priors","page":"Physics","text":"get_priors(cfg::XsecModel) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturn prior distributions for each cross-section normalization parameter.\n\nAll priors are truncated normal distributions centred at 1.0.\n\nArguments\n\ncfg::XsecModel: a SimpleScaling or Differential_H2O instance.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple mapping parameter names to Distributions.Truncated{Normal} priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.xsec.get_priors"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.xsec.get_scale","page":"Physics","text":"get_scale(cfg::XsecModel) -> Function\n\nConstruct the cross-section scaling closure for the given model.\n\nSimpleScaling returns a function with signature:\n\nscale(flav::Symbol, interaction::Symbol, params::NamedTuple) -> Real\n\nReturns params.nc_norm for NC interactions, params.nutau_cc_norm for nu_tau CC, and 1.0 for everything else.\n\nDifferential_H2O returns a function with signature:\n\nscale(E::AbstractArray, flav::Symbol, interaction::Symbol, anti::Bool, params::NamedTuple) -> Real or AbstractArray\n\nFor NC interactions returns params.nc_norm. For CC interactions computes an energy-dependent weighted sum of per-mode normalizations using interpolated interaction fractions, with an additional nu_tau CC factor when applicable.\n\nArguments\n\ncfg::XsecModel: cross-section model instance.\n\nReturns\n\nA closure computing the cross-section scale factor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.xsec.get_scale"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.Helpers.bin","page":"Physics","text":"bin(df, out_centers; col=1)\n\nBins a list of events in DataFrame or Matrix df into out_centers using the specified column (col) for the observable. Returns a vector of counts per bin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.Helpers.bin"},{"category":"function","location":"api/physics/#Newtrinos.Helpers.rebin","page":"Physics","text":"rebin(df, out_centers; var_col=1, count_col=2)\n\nRebins a table of (observable, count) pairs in DataFrame or Matrix df into out_centers. var_col specifies which column contains the observable to bin in. count_col specifies which column contains the counts. Returns a vector of summed counts per bin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Newtrinos.Helpers.rebin"},{"category":"section","location":"#Newtrinos.jl","page":"Home","text":"Newtrinos.jl is a Julia package for the global analysis of neutrino data, fully open source and free to use under the MIT license.","title":"Newtrinos.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Overview","page":"Home","text":"The package is built to support flexible and modular analysis of neutrino physics, combining experimental data with physics models and inference tools. It supports both Frequentist (profile likelihood) and Bayesian (nested sampling, importance sampling) inference methods.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#Key-Features","page":"Home","text":"11 experiments: IceCube DeepCore, Daya Bay, KamLAND, MINOS, Super-Kamiokande, ORCA, JUNO, TAO, COHERENT CsI/LAr, IceCube Upgrade\nModular physics: Oscillations (3-flavour, sterile, ADD, dark dimensions), matter effects (SI, NSI), atmospheric fluxes, cross-sections\nFully differentiable: All code supports ForwardDiff automatic differentiation for gradient-based optimization\nScalable: Threaded and distributed parallelism for profile likelihood scans","title":"Key Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Example","page":"Home","text":"using Newtrinos\nusing DensityInterface\n\n# Configure experiments with default physics\nexperiments = (\n    deepcore = Newtrinos.deepcore.configure(),\n    dayabay  = Newtrinos.dayabay.configure(),\n)\n\n# Build joint likelihood\nlikelihood = Newtrinos.generate_likelihood(experiments)\nparams = Newtrinos.get_params(experiments)\n\n# Evaluate\nlogdensityof(likelihood, params)","title":"Quick Example"},{"category":"section","location":"#References","page":"Home","text":"Newtrinos.jl has been used to produce the results presented in:\n\nTesting the number of neutrino species with a global fit of neutrino data — Phys.Rev.D 109 (2024) 9, 095016\nConstraints on non-unitary neutrino mixing in light of atmospheric and reactor neutrino data — JHEP 05 (2025) 130\nA neutrino data analysis of extra-dimensional theories with massive bulk fields — Phys.Rev.D 112 (2025) 5, 055009","title":"References"}]
}
